WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.458
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast, where

00:00:02.458 --> 00:00:06.421
we discuss science and science
based tools for everyday life.

00:00:06.421 --> 00:00:09.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:10.900
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.060
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.770
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.770 --> 00:00:17.230
Today we are
discussing creativity.

00:00:17.230 --> 00:00:21.310
Creativity is a topic that to
many people is very abstract.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:25.330
That is, we know when something
seems creative, some of us

00:00:25.330 --> 00:00:28.570
know people who are creative
or perhaps are creative,

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:32.619
and yet the ability to be
creative resides in everybody.

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.720
And we know that because
the neural circuits

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.200
that underlie creativity
have been somewhat defined

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.080
and the steps and processes
within the brain and body that

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.570
lead to creativity
are well known.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:47.450
That said, most people don't
know how to access creativity.

00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:49.660
And if they do know how
to access creativity,

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:51.610
they are only able
to access creativity

00:00:51.610 --> 00:00:54.040
in a fairly limited
number of domains of life.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.750
For instance, in the visual arts
or in music or within science

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.080
or engineering or any number
of different domains ranging

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.210
from the kitchen to sport
to childhood interactions.

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:09.140
That is, childhood games.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:11.350
In other words, some
adults are able to access

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.140
their creative spirit when
engaging in childlike play

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.230
with children or for
that matter with adults.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.990
But as it turns out,
all of creativity

00:01:19.990 --> 00:01:22.600
stems from just a small
subset of neural structures

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:24.850
in the brain that
need to be activated

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.680
in a particular
sequence or order.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.170
Today we will talk about what
those neural structures are,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:32.380
what particular order they need
to be activated in in order

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:35.650
to come up with, for instance,
new ideas that are creative,

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:39.130
and then how to implement
those creative strategies.

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:40.930
We will also talk
about different ways

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:43.240
to access creativity
that include--

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:47.260
narrative and storytelling as
well as applying new rule sets

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:49.120
or even entirely new worldviews.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:51.550
And we will do this in
a structured way that

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.070
will allow anyone, whether
or not you consider yourself

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.740
creative or not to
be able to apply

00:01:56.740 --> 00:02:00.550
these tools in different domains
of life-- work, family, play,

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:01.660
and on and on.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:03.220
By the end of
today's episode, you

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.730
will have a better understanding
of what creativity is

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.479
and how to access it.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:12.610
And if you like to bring others
into your creative endeavors

00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:15.430
which as you'll soon
learn can massively

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:17.770
expand the extent to
which you yourself

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.690
can express your
creative talents.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.930
As is the case with all episodes
of the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:02:22.930 --> 00:02:25.630
today we will discuss
both scientific mechanisms

00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:26.560
and nomenclature.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.483
And I promise to make
all of that clear to you

00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:30.400
even if you don't have
a background in biology

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.630
or psychology.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.970
But we will also, of
course, discuss tools.

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.310
That is, specific steps
that you can take in order

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:37.420
to be more creative.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:39.910
One particular tool that I'm
excited to share with you

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.810
involves a meditation, but this
is a very unusual meditation.

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:46.660
This is not sitting with eyes
closed focusing on your breath

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.600
or focusing on a chime
or some other feature

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:51.970
in your sensory environment
or even in your body.

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:55.750
Later we will talk about
open monitoring meditations.

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.080
Open monitoring meditations are
very distinct from other forms

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.510
of meditation and
involve learning

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:04.090
how to sit back
and simply observe

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:06.700
your thoughts
while intentionally

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:08.690
varying where your thoughts go.

00:03:08.690 --> 00:03:11.170
So for those of you
that find it a struggle

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.980
to focus or to refocus in more
traditional forms of meditation

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:17.920
or maybe even in your work
and even for those of you that

00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:21.670
may suffer from things
like ADHD or similar,

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:25.210
open monitoring meditation can
be an extremely valuable tool

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:26.973
for accessing your
creative abilities.

00:03:26.973 --> 00:03:28.390
Because of the
ways that it allows

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.400
you to tap into
specific circuits

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.180
within the frontal
networks of your brain,

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.805
so these are networks
of the brain that

00:03:33.805 --> 00:03:36.400
include the areas just
behind your forehead

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.510
and that allow you to evaluate
new and novel rule sets

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:41.892
in a very unconstrained way.

00:03:41.892 --> 00:03:43.600
Because if you think
about it, creativity

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:46.120
is really the ability to
take existing elements

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.050
from the physical world or from
the thought world, if you will,

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:54.850
or from any domain of life,
mood, thinking, and information

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:58.000
and to reorder those into
novel combinations that

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.710
are useful for something.

00:03:59.710 --> 00:04:01.270
And as we'll also
find out later,

00:04:01.270 --> 00:04:03.800
creativity has this
incredible aspect to it,

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.380
which is that when we see or
create or experience something

00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:11.800
that is truly creative,
it reveals to us something

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.440
fundamental about the way
that the natural world

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.959
and indeed the way
that our brains work.

00:04:16.959 --> 00:04:19.300
If that sounds very mysterious
and abstract to you now,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.529
I promise that by the
end of today's episode,

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.470
you will not only
understand what that means

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.320
but you will also understand
how to use open monitoring

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:29.860
meditations as well as other
forms of tools in order

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.450
to access your creative ability.

00:04:31.450 --> 00:04:33.162
Before we begin, I'd
