00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:53.660
Let's talk about creativity.

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:57.320
Now on the face of it, the word
creativity and creative acts

00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.270
might seem somewhat
abstract to us.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:01.670
That is, we know
when we see something

00:09:01.670 --> 00:09:05.300
that we consider creative and
we know when we see something

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:06.740
that is not creative.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.330
Things that aren't
creative are things

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:11.660
that we see every day, a car
with four tires, for instance,

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:15.230
a bicycle with two
tires, not creative.

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:19.013
However, we also see things that
are novel that are different

00:09:19.013 --> 00:09:20.930
and that we don't really
think of as creative.

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:23.120
In fact, they can be
downright trivial.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:25.610
For instance, if I were
to take a fish tank

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:29.690
and put wings on it,
that's a novel combination

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.360
of things, which is
one of the key criteria

00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:36.620
for an act or an object
or a piece of music

00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:37.760
that is creative.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:40.520
And yet, neither of
us I believe would

00:09:40.520 --> 00:09:42.740
find it very creative
or very interesting

00:09:42.740 --> 00:09:44.120
that a fish tank
has wings on it.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.440
Why or why not I should say?

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:48.470
Well, it turns out
that for something

00:09:48.470 --> 00:09:51.080
to be creative it actually
has to reveal to us something

00:09:51.080 --> 00:09:54.810
fundamental about the
world or about how we work.

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:58.340
And I must say that oftentimes
the most creative and the most

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.520
interesting and the most
beloved creative acts

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.950
reveal to us something
fundamental about the world

00:10:03.950 --> 00:10:06.830
or the way that we work in a
way that delights and thrills

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:09.950
and surprise prizes us but
that we aren't even aware what

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.208
that fundamental rule is.

00:10:11.208 --> 00:10:12.750
I'll return to this
in a few minutes.

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:16.010
But for the time being,
let's just build up

00:10:16.010 --> 00:10:18.710
from first principles what
constitutes something creative

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:22.154
and what does not constitute
something creative.

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:24.980
Creativity is a way of
interacting with the world

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:27.530
or combining or recombining
things in the world

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:31.640
in a way that appears novel
to us and to other people.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:35.570
My example of a fish tank
with wings on it is novel.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.370
But frankly, it's
not very creative

00:10:37.370 --> 00:10:39.020
and it's not very interesting.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:41.720
It doesn't reveal
anything new to us.

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:44.810
Sure there are flying fish
although they just jump far,

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.020
they don't really fly.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.920
And as a consequence,
putting wings on a fish tank

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:51.643
could be used as a metaphor for
the fact that fish don't fly.

00:10:51.643 --> 00:10:53.060
But you already
knew and I already

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.440
knew that fish don't fly.

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.180
And so there's
nothing novel revealed

00:10:56.180 --> 00:10:59.270
to us about the world except
something we already knew.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:02.150
Now, creative acts on the
other hand, of course,

00:11:02.150 --> 00:11:06.020
involve novel combinations
of existing rule sets that

00:11:06.020 --> 00:11:09.080
could be different combinations
of music or colors or shapes

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:12.410
or technology, et cetera.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:15.350
But it does so in a way
that tells us something

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:17.330
fundamental and different.

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:21.530
Let me give an example of a few
truly creative artistic acts.

00:11:21.530 --> 00:11:25.393
And I'll do that in the domain
of visual arts but, of course,

00:11:25.393 --> 00:11:26.810
there are many
examples that could

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:30.230
come from music or from other
domains sport, et cetera.

00:11:30.230 --> 00:11:33.590
The examples I'll give rather
than a fish tank with wings

00:11:33.590 --> 00:11:38.390
are for instance, the
comparison between a drawing

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:44.060
or a very accurate painting
of a face, an Escher

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.790
painting, and a Banksy.

00:11:46.790 --> 00:11:49.310
If you don't know what
those are I'll explain.

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:52.430
First of all, let's talk about
an accurate representation

00:11:52.430 --> 00:11:53.210
of a face.

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:54.920
If I were to sit
you down or if you

00:11:54.920 --> 00:11:56.900
were to send me a
photograph and then

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:59.030
I were to paint
or draw a picture

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:02.210
of your face in a way that
faithfully represented,

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:05.180
the position and shape of your
nose relative to the eyes,

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:09.410
maybe a curl of the
lip, maybe a few hairs

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:11.660
of your eyebrows in a
particular way, that

00:12:11.660 --> 00:12:14.060
really captured you accurately.

00:12:14.060 --> 00:12:16.970
I think most people would
say, OK, it's accurate.

00:12:16.970 --> 00:12:19.040
It looks a lot like the
photograph or the person.

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:22.310
And on the one hand why
that could be interesting

00:12:22.310 --> 00:12:24.950
it's not particularly
creative because it faithfully

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.770
represents what's already there.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:31.280
In contrast, a painting or
a picture like an Escher,

00:12:31.280 --> 00:12:34.310
and for those of you that aren't
familiar with Escher's involves

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:35.580
a lot of repeating patterns.

00:12:35.580 --> 00:12:38.540
So for instance, a bird image
that's repeated over and over

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:40.130
and over and over
again, sometimes

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:42.470
in partially overlapping manner.

00:12:42.470 --> 00:12:45.470
And perhaps a building
that's repeated over and over

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:47.780
and over again or stones
repeated over and over again

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.270
or staircases over
and over again.

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:54.620
Escher's capture elements
from the outside world

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.620
and faithfully represent them,
but faithfully represent them

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:00.230
over and over and over again,
which is not typically seen

00:13:00.230 --> 00:13:01.140
in the natural world.

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:03.830
In fact, most of what
our visual system does

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:07.020
is to eliminate repetitive
patterns when we see them.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:08.960
In fact, most of what
our visual system does

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:11.450
is try and make us blind
to repetitive patterns

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:13.430
in our visual
environment and only

00:13:13.430 --> 00:13:15.650
allow us to see things
that are unusual

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:17.270
in that visual environment.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.092
Now, this is especially
true at visual scales.

00:13:21.092 --> 00:13:23.300
What I mean by that is if
you were to go to the beach

00:13:23.300 --> 00:13:25.550
and lie on your towel and
look down at the sand,

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.340
you would start to notice
that the sand is a very, very

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.180
repetitive pattern.

00:13:29.180 --> 00:13:32.150
So at very small scales and in
particular at molecular scales

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:34.970
when you get down to the
level of atoms and so forth,

00:13:34.970 --> 00:13:36.075
everything is repetitive.

00:13:36.075 --> 00:13:37.700
It's the same thing,
is just reproduced

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.320
in different combinations
over and over again.

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:40.610
But as we move
through our world,

00:13:40.610 --> 00:13:42.985
typically we're not looking
down at pebbles on the ground

00:13:42.985 --> 00:13:46.730
or little grains of sand
or the pattern of leaves

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:48.973
in a particular clover or
something of that sort.

00:13:48.973 --> 00:13:50.390
Most of the time
we're looking out

00:13:50.390 --> 00:13:52.940
on landscapes or at
people's faces, et cetera.

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:57.410
And very seldom do we see
highly repetitive patterns

00:13:57.410 --> 00:13:58.650
at that scale.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:14:00.800
So what Escher's do
is they essentially

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:02.810
reveal to us a
fundamental feature

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:04.980
about the way that our
visual system works,

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:09.170
which is that repetitive
patterns tend to become noise

00:14:09.170 --> 00:14:10.100
in our visual system.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:13.160
That is, our brain encodes
repetition as things

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:14.690
not to be interested in.

00:14:14.690 --> 00:14:17.180
And the things that stand
out against that repetition

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:20.450
as the things to be interested
in so-called signal to noise.

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:23.660
What Escher's do is they invert
the relationship between signal

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:26.720
and noise and they make the
repetitive patterns the signal

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:29.540
and the unusual
patterns the noise.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.760
In fact, in every Escher
there are unusual patterns

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:33.560
and those completely
disappear to us.

00:14:33.560 --> 00:14:35.810
Now, when you look at an
Escher, what you probably see

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.435
and what I see are just a bunch
of birds repeated over and over

00:14:38.435 --> 00:14:41.170
again or buildings or staircases
repeated over and over again.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:42.920
And you may like
Escher's and you may not,

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:43.880
that's not the point.

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:45.500
Today we're not
talking about taste

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:47.120
in particular creative acts.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:48.800
What we're trying
to identify here

00:14:48.800 --> 00:14:51.650
are the rules and mechanisms
of what constitutes something

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:53.420
creative and why it's creative.

00:14:53.420 --> 00:14:56.870
And the key element here is that
what's revealed by an Escher

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.360
through these
repetition patterns

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.550
is an inversion of the way
that our brain normally

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.960
encodes visual images.

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:06.110
And therefore, the
rule that repetition

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:08.780
is suppressed in
our visual system

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:14.480
and that unusual visual features
are revealed to us, that rule

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:16.853
is what pops out to us
when we look at an Escher.

00:15:16.853 --> 00:15:19.520
Now, when I say pops out I don't
mean that you look at an Escher

00:15:19.520 --> 00:15:21.590
and go oh, normally I
don't see repetition,

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.200
normally I see the unusual
stuff, et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:24.200 --> 00:15:27.260
But there seems to be something
about truly creative acts

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:29.030
that capture the attention.

00:15:29.030 --> 00:15:31.670
And sometimes the delight
of many, many people

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:34.340
is that they reveal a
fundamental rule about how

00:15:34.340 --> 00:15:36.300
the brain or the world works.

00:15:36.300 --> 00:15:38.460
Let me give you a
different example

00:15:38.460 --> 00:15:40.290
also from the visual art world.

00:15:40.290 --> 00:15:42.110
Let me give you the
example of Banksy.

00:15:42.110 --> 00:15:45.500
Banksy is an artist that many of
you are probably familiar with

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.690
and probably some of you
are not familiar with.

00:15:47.690 --> 00:15:49.940
So for those of you that are
not familiar with Banksy,

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.490
Banksy is an artist
that most often

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:53.900
does two dimensional artwork.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.750
So these would be stencils
or paintings or drawings

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:59.930
like many artists and does
them in an urban landscape,

00:15:59.930 --> 00:16:03.410
an actual city or
suburban landscape.

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:08.040
That is, he draws or stencils or
graffiti in a very cryptic way,

00:16:08.040 --> 00:16:08.840
I should say.

00:16:08.840 --> 00:16:11.540
No one really knows who Banksy
is or when he does his art.

00:16:11.540 --> 00:16:13.250
He just reveals his
art by putting it up.

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:15.800
But he does this in
the context of cities

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:17.640
and on three
dimensional objects.

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:21.620
So a good example would be he
will stencil next to a phone

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:23.390
booth a police officer.

00:16:23.390 --> 00:16:27.230
Or he will graffiti next
to an actual fire hydrant

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:30.560
a dog lifting its leg to
urinate on that fire hydrant.

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.670
Now, what's interesting about
Banksy's is not simply the fact

00:16:34.670 --> 00:16:37.970
that he puts two dimensional art
onto three dimensional surfaces

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:40.030
in the urban and
suburban landscape

00:16:40.030 --> 00:16:41.780
because if you think
about it, that's been

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:43.070
done many, many times before.

00:16:43.070 --> 00:16:44.450
All graffiti is that.

00:16:44.450 --> 00:16:48.050
All City Art and murals is that.

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:49.880
So what's unique about Banksy?

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:52.760
What's unique about
Banksy or I should

00:16:52.760 --> 00:16:56.660
say Banksy's, the actual
art, is that he combines

00:16:56.660 --> 00:17:01.340
two dimensional art with a three
dimensional landscape in a way

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:04.292
that the concept
pops out at you.

00:17:04.292 --> 00:17:05.250
What do I mean by that?

00:17:05.250 --> 00:17:07.083
Well, in the case of
the dog lifting its leg

00:17:07.083 --> 00:17:09.770
to urinate on the fire hydrant,
that's a scene that most people

00:17:09.770 --> 00:17:12.200
and in fact, most children are
familiar with from cartoons

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:14.575
or from our basic understanding
of the stereotype of dogs

00:17:14.575 --> 00:17:17.660
and I must tell you having
owned a male dog, a bulldog

00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:20.089
Costello for many years.

00:17:20.089 --> 00:17:22.595
Hydrants were a particular
target for Costello.

00:17:22.595 --> 00:17:24.470
Of course, everything
was a particular target

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:26.569
for Costello urinating outdoors.

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:29.330
Nonetheless, he liked
to pee on fire hydrants.

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:31.100
That itself is not interesting.

00:17:31.100 --> 00:17:33.650
Seeing a photograph of
a dog raising its leg

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:37.040
to pee on a fire hydrant
is not interesting.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.800
Seeing a painting of
that isn't interesting.

00:17:39.800 --> 00:17:42.200
Seeing an actual dog
urinating on a fire hydrant

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:43.160
isn't interesting.

00:17:43.160 --> 00:17:47.870
In fact, seeing a painting in
two dimensions of a dog raising

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:50.510
its leg to, of course,
it can't actually

00:17:50.510 --> 00:17:53.330
urinate but give you the
impression that it would

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:55.850
urinate on that fire
hydrant isn't particularly

00:17:55.850 --> 00:17:58.580
interesting except for the fact
that it reveals to us something

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:00.320
fundamental, which
is that we tend

00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:04.100
to pair visual relationships
between different objects

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.620
that share a common
theme and then

00:18:06.620 --> 00:18:07.975
the theme tends to pop out us.

00:18:07.975 --> 00:18:09.350
So, for instance,
the dog raising

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:10.928
its leg next to a
fire hydrant even

00:18:10.928 --> 00:18:12.470
if the dog is drawn
in two dimensions

00:18:12.470 --> 00:18:14.390
and the fire hydrant
is in three dimensions

00:18:14.390 --> 00:18:17.120
allows the concept of
dog and fire hydrant

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.210
to emerge or pop out at us,
which reveals to us something

00:18:20.210 --> 00:18:22.080
fundamental about
how our brain works,

00:18:22.080 --> 00:18:25.910
which is that our brain encodes
concepts and entire stories

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:29.583
as symbols of interaction
between different objects.

00:18:29.583 --> 00:18:31.250
Let me give you a
different example just

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:33.860
to make sure that
this hits home.

00:18:33.860 --> 00:18:36.050
One of Banksy's more
famous paintings

00:18:36.050 --> 00:18:38.060
is a rather politically
charged one,

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:42.650
which is of a girl holding
a bouquet of balloons.

00:18:42.650 --> 00:18:47.210
And this two dimensional drawing
was put on to the West wall

00:18:47.210 --> 00:18:50.030
dividing territories
in the Middle East.

00:18:50.030 --> 00:18:51.752
A very controversial issue.

00:18:51.752 --> 00:18:53.210
The controversies
of that issue are

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:54.200
not what I want to get into.

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:55.760
But I don't think
anyone would doubt

00:18:55.760 --> 00:18:58.100
that is a controversial issue.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:18:59.900
The two dimensional
drawing of the girl

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.050
with the balloons
on the actual wall

00:19:03.050 --> 00:19:06.920
turns out to be quite
interesting as an art piece

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:10.400
because what it reveals to
us is the entire controversy

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.860
around the presence of
that wall and the desire

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:15.230
for certain people to breach
that wall and the desire

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:17.967
for other people to insist
that that wall not be breached

00:19:17.967 --> 00:19:18.800
for whatever reason.

00:19:18.800 --> 00:19:21.080
Again, this is not about
the particular controversy,

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:24.080
the point is that a two
dimensional image combined

00:19:24.080 --> 00:19:25.880
with a three
dimensional structure

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:29.960
allows the purpose of that
three dimensional structure.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:32.750
And the controversy around that
three dimensional structure

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:36.470
to pop out at us in a way that
if, for instance, we had just

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:39.650
seen a photograph of
somebody next to that wall

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:41.600
or with a ladder
or if we just seen

00:19:41.600 --> 00:19:45.860
a drawing of a girl holding
a bouquet of balloons

00:19:45.860 --> 00:19:48.470
on a drawing of that
wall to not emerge.

00:19:48.470 --> 00:19:50.715
In other words, it captures
two fundamental features

00:19:50.715 --> 00:19:51.590
of the visual system.

00:19:51.590 --> 00:19:53.810
Our ability to encode
things in two dimensions

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:55.160
and understand symbols.

00:19:55.160 --> 00:19:57.855
And our ability to understand
things in three dimensions.

00:19:57.855 --> 00:20:00.230
And in particular, the wall
as a three dimensional object

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:02.450
is really interesting
because it's

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:04.110
an actual physical barrier.

00:20:04.110 --> 00:20:06.230
So showing it as the
actual physical barrier

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:09.170
that it is in real space,
in three dimensions

00:20:09.170 --> 00:20:12.300
turns out to allow the
interaction between those two

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:12.800
things.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:16.610
The concept, the controversy
to pop out at us and make us

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:18.800
think about that particular
controversy and perhaps

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.980
where we each individually
stand on that controversy.

00:20:21.980 --> 00:20:24.260
Now, there are many
examples of what I just

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.120
gave in the visual domain.

00:20:26.120 --> 00:20:28.700
For instance, Rothko's
which are just

00:20:28.700 --> 00:20:32.300
color on Canvas are
particularly interesting source

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:35.030
of information about the way
that the brain encodes color.

00:20:35.030 --> 00:20:37.700
Later I'll fill in exactly
what that information is.

00:20:37.700 --> 00:20:39.233
You may like
Rothko's, you may not.

00:20:39.233 --> 00:20:40.400
But I'll tell you one thing.

00:20:40.400 --> 00:20:42.950
When you look at
a Rothko, you are

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:45.110
seeing colors in a
very different way

00:20:45.110 --> 00:20:47.870
than you would ever see
colors in any other context.

00:20:47.870 --> 00:20:50.360
The fact that they don't
have a frame typically

00:20:50.360 --> 00:20:53.780
and the fact that
there's no white canvas

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:55.850
allows the colors
that you see to be

00:20:55.850 --> 00:20:58.490
novel hues of those
colors that you will not

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:00.290
see in any other context.

00:21:00.290 --> 00:21:03.620
And in doing so
reveals to you what

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:07.580
your brain does in order to
understand and extract color.

00:21:07.580 --> 00:21:09.800
Now in the context of
music, for instance,

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:13.280
you will sometimes hear a
street musician play a song

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:16.130
maybe a Bob Dylan song or a Led
Zeppelin song or a Pink Floyd

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:18.987
song pretty closely, pretty
accurately to the way

00:21:18.987 --> 00:21:19.820
that song is played.

00:21:19.820 --> 00:21:20.870
But, of course,
that's not creative.

00:21:20.870 --> 00:21:23.203
That's just like the photograph
or the accurate portrait

00:21:23.203 --> 00:21:24.050
of somebody's face.

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:26.000
Or you may hear an
acoustic version

00:21:26.000 --> 00:21:29.510
of what's normally an electric
guitar song or electrical song

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:30.590
or vise versa.

00:21:30.590 --> 00:21:32.480
Somewhat creative,
sometimes sounds

00:21:32.480 --> 00:21:35.030
even better than the original
but not particularly creative.

00:21:35.030 --> 00:21:37.310
However, each and
every one of us

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.930
has a particular taste in music.

00:21:38.930 --> 00:21:41.222
Maybe it's classical, maybe
it's rock, maybe it's punk,

00:21:41.222 --> 00:21:42.430
maybe it's hip hop.

00:21:42.430 --> 00:21:45.070
Within each of those
genres, I think all of us

00:21:45.070 --> 00:21:48.520
are familiar with hearing
something for the first time

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.110
and maybe even every time.

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:52.420
And there's something
about the combination

00:21:52.420 --> 00:21:55.150
of the words and the
music or sometimes

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:58.780
just the music or just the words
that allows some feature of it

00:21:58.780 --> 00:22:02.170
to pop out at us as
particularly exciting.

00:22:02.170 --> 00:22:03.910
And when we feel that
excitement and we

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.470
feel that it's
really novel, it's

00:22:05.470 --> 00:22:07.780
different than what we've
heard before, I assure you

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:10.120
what it's revealing
to you is the way

00:22:10.120 --> 00:22:12.010
that your auditory
system and often

00:22:12.010 --> 00:22:14.110
your auditory and
your emotional system

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.960
encodes information
that you hear.

00:22:16.960 --> 00:22:19.510
And again, the rule
that it's revealing

00:22:19.510 --> 00:22:21.530
is not splayed out for you.

00:22:21.530 --> 00:22:23.020
For instance, it's
not told to you

00:22:23.020 --> 00:22:24.370
oh, this is the way
you normally hear

00:22:24.370 --> 00:22:25.990
and now you're hearing
things differently.

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:27.700
Sometimes it's the
change in for instance

00:22:27.700 --> 00:22:29.200
in the way that
words are accented

00:22:29.200 --> 00:22:31.210
or the way the sentences
are constructed.

00:22:31.210 --> 00:22:32.950
This often you'll hear
in hip hop the way

00:22:32.950 --> 00:22:35.740
that sentences are
constructed can be divided up

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:37.668
not as normal declarative
sentences the way

00:22:37.668 --> 00:22:38.960
that they're typically written.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:41.860
But the way that sentences
are chopped up and fractured

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.492
reveals to us new meaning
and in fact, enhanced

00:22:44.492 --> 00:22:46.450
meaning about particular
words that we wouldn't

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:48.200
see if it was written
out as a paragraph

00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:50.810
and then sung as a script that
would be the same as the one

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:51.610
that we would read.

00:22:51.610 --> 00:22:55.660
Again, the point is that what
is exciting and novel to you is

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:56.950
just the way that you hear it.

00:22:56.950 --> 00:22:58.600
But it's exciting
and novel to you

00:22:58.600 --> 00:23:02.080
because there are circuits
within the brain that when

00:23:02.080 --> 00:23:06.400
we hear or see or
feel or experience

00:23:06.400 --> 00:23:10.780
known elements in new ways
that are truly creative,

00:23:10.780 --> 00:23:14.830
the way that those neural
circuits function is changed.

00:23:14.830 --> 00:23:17.110
And when neural
circuits change the way

00:23:17.110 --> 00:23:19.570
that they function
simply by way of what

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:21.550
comes into our eyes,
our ears, and the way

00:23:21.550 --> 00:23:23.950
that we experience
our feelings, there

00:23:23.950 --> 00:23:25.660
is the release of
chemicals, including

00:23:25.660 --> 00:23:27.190
the release of the
chemical dopamine

00:23:27.190 --> 00:23:29.140
and other
neuromodulators as well

00:23:29.140 --> 00:23:32.180
that make us feel both
surprised, delighted.

00:23:32.180 --> 00:23:35.890
And this is very key,
excited in anticipation

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.605
that we might see it again.

00:23:37.605 --> 00:23:38.980
So with the
understanding in mind

00:23:38.980 --> 00:23:41.800
that true creativity
involves the l combination

00:23:41.800 --> 00:23:46.450
of some elements could be notes
of music, could be numbers,

00:23:46.450 --> 00:23:49.210
could be visual elements
like lines or colors,

00:23:49.210 --> 00:23:51.520
could be physical
movements, et cetera.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:54.580
But novel combinations
of some things

00:23:54.580 --> 00:23:58.030
that reveal to us something
fundamental about the way

00:23:58.030 --> 00:24:00.470
that our brain and/or
the world work.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:02.650
And of course, as
I mentioned before,

00:24:02.650 --> 00:24:04.780
that fundamental
thing may or may not

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.060
be consciously accessible to us.

00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:10.900
We may not know what exactly
it is that's novel to us.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:14.620
But it feels novel
and it feels true.

00:24:14.620 --> 00:24:16.360
Well, with that
understanding in mind,

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.330
we therefore can ask,
what are the underlying

00:24:19.330 --> 00:24:21.400
principles and
neural circuits that

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:23.410
underlie the creative process.

00:24:23.410 --> 00:24:26.450
And the word process here
is especially important.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:28.300
In fact, if there's
one thing I'd really

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.910
like to impress on
everybody is that when

00:24:30.910 --> 00:24:34.090
thinking about biology,
it's almost always better

00:24:34.090 --> 00:24:36.490
to think about verbs
as opposed to nouns.

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:38.320
So rather than
think of creativity

00:24:38.320 --> 00:24:41.830
as a noun or somebody being
creative as an adjective,

00:24:41.830 --> 00:24:43.570
think about the verb creativity.

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:45.820
That is what are
the steps required

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:48.290
and therefore what are the
cells and circuits and thoughts,

00:24:48.290 --> 00:24:52.150
et cetera required in
order to be creative.

00:24:52.150 --> 00:24:54.910
This element of
thinking about verbs

00:24:54.910 --> 00:24:58.510
then allows us to say, OK, what
are the various steps in coming

00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:01.930
up with a creative idea,
in testing a creative idea,

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:04.180
and then implementing
that creative idea.

00:25:04.180 --> 00:25:07.660
And in doing so, we find based
on the scientific literature

00:25:07.660 --> 00:25:09.970
that there are basically
three major networks

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:12.940
within the brain, each of
which is responsible for each

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:16.330
of the three steps to arrive
at something truly creative.

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:18.370
The first neural circuit
involved in creativity

00:25:18.370 --> 00:25:20.770
is the so-called
executive network.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:23.710
This is a goofy name because
the neural circuits that I'm

00:25:23.710 --> 00:25:26.110
about to describe do a bunch
of other things as well,

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:29.620
but they certainly control what
are called executive functions.

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:31.900
Executive functions
are functions

00:25:31.900 --> 00:25:35.350
that you and I both have,
which is our ability

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:37.480
to govern our thinking
and our behavior

00:25:37.480 --> 00:25:38.950
in very deliberate ways.

00:25:38.950 --> 00:25:42.010
And that is largely
accomplished through the use

00:25:42.010 --> 00:25:44.290
of the neural circuitry
that sits right

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:46.850
behind the forebrain, the
so-called prefrontal cortex.

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:49.180
Now, the prefrontal
cortex involves

00:25:49.180 --> 00:25:50.565
many different subregions.

00:25:50.565 --> 00:25:51.940
It has a bunch of
different parts

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:54.148
just like any country has
different states, et cetera

00:25:54.148 --> 00:25:55.720
and provinces.

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.000
Executive function involves
the prefrontal cortex

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:00.140
and some other
neural structures.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:02.650
But for the sake
of this discussion,

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:05.590
executive function and
the prefrontal cortex

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:08.710
are mainly responsible
for suppressing action.

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:13.300
That is, for eliminating choices
among the infinite number

00:26:13.300 --> 00:26:14.960
of choices that exist.

00:26:14.960 --> 00:26:17.260
For instance, of what colors
to combine on a painting

00:26:17.260 --> 00:26:19.660
or what lines to draw
or what notes to play

00:26:19.660 --> 00:26:21.340
or what movements
to make in a sports

00:26:21.340 --> 00:26:23.680
endeavor, what numbers to
include in a mathematics

00:26:23.680 --> 00:26:27.040
endeavor, or what words
and letters and syllables

00:26:27.040 --> 00:26:31.950
and sentences to include in
writing a creative passage.

00:26:31.950 --> 00:26:35.162
The second network is the
so-called default mode network.

00:26:35.162 --> 00:26:37.620
There's a lot of discussion
nowadays about the default mode

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:40.380
network as it relates to
consciousness and meditation,

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:40.920
et cetera.

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:43.540
The default mode network
does many different things.

00:26:43.540 --> 00:26:45.930
But in the context of our
discussion about creativity,

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:48.300
the default mode
network is really

00:26:48.300 --> 00:26:50.160
the network that
starts being engaged

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:53.100
when you close your eyes
and start paying attention

00:26:53.100 --> 00:26:56.130
to what's going on in terms
of your thinking as opposed

00:26:56.130 --> 00:26:58.380
to the sensory outside world.

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:02.100
And the default mode network is
especially important for what's

00:27:02.100 --> 00:27:04.030
called spontaneous imagination.

00:27:04.030 --> 00:27:05.940
Now, spontaneous
imagination is something

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.550
that you can try at any moment
if you were to close your eyes

00:27:08.550 --> 00:27:11.580
and to try and not pay attention
to the sounds around you.

00:27:11.580 --> 00:27:16.160
But even if you do, to just
pay attention to whatever

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:19.720
thoughts or feelings emerge
when your eyes are closed.

00:27:19.720 --> 00:27:21.470
By closing your eyes
and shutting yourself

00:27:21.470 --> 00:27:24.180
off to the outside
sensory world,

00:27:24.180 --> 00:27:27.290
you start to engage much
more of your brain machinery

00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:29.120
dedicated towards
what's going on

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.460
inside you, so-called
interoception but also

00:27:31.460 --> 00:27:33.380
what you're thinking
about your thinking

00:27:33.380 --> 00:27:36.000
whether or not your thoughts
are complete or incomplete,

00:27:36.000 --> 00:27:38.690
whether or not they are
fragmentary in a way that goes

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:41.270
from one thought to another
distantly in the past

00:27:41.270 --> 00:27:42.890
or present to future, et cetera.

00:27:42.890 --> 00:27:44.885
Depending on time of
day, how well-rested

00:27:44.885 --> 00:27:47.570
you are, how stressed you
are, how happy you are,

00:27:47.570 --> 00:27:49.368
the default mode
network will take you

00:27:49.368 --> 00:27:51.410
through a journey of
different types of thoughts,

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:53.035
different types of
feelings, et cetera.

00:27:53.035 --> 00:27:55.070
The specific types of
thoughts and feelings

00:27:55.070 --> 00:27:56.540
are not as interesting
as the fact

00:27:56.540 --> 00:27:58.400
that when you close your
eyes you're essentially

00:27:58.400 --> 00:28:00.800
engaging this default mode
network, which is essentially

00:28:00.800 --> 00:28:04.040
the network associated with
imagination and imagination

00:28:04.040 --> 00:28:07.880
based on elements that exist
only within your head, that

00:28:07.880 --> 00:28:13.010
is within your brain and
therefore must rely on memory

00:28:13.010 --> 00:28:14.685
of previous experiences.

00:28:14.685 --> 00:28:17.060
As soon as you close your
eyes, you are shutting yourself

00:28:17.060 --> 00:28:18.540
off from the sensory world.

00:28:18.540 --> 00:28:20.390
So by definition
you can no longer

00:28:20.390 --> 00:28:23.120
be bringing in novel
experiences in that moment.

00:28:23.120 --> 00:28:26.300
You're relying on your library
of existing experiences

00:28:26.300 --> 00:28:29.450
and your memory of those in
order to imagine new things.

00:28:29.450 --> 00:28:31.963
And you're doing this in a
very free associative way.

00:28:31.963 --> 00:28:33.630
You're not trying to
imagine new things.

00:28:33.630 --> 00:28:35.700
It's just whatever
geysers to the surface.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:37.160
So we've got the
executive network,

00:28:37.160 --> 00:28:41.120
which is involved in suppressing
particular thoughts or actions.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:43.370
We have the default
mode network, which

00:28:43.370 --> 00:28:44.780
is involved in imagination.

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:47.060
And the default mode network
I should mention also

00:28:47.060 --> 00:28:48.990
involves a subregion of
the prefrontal cortex.

00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:50.657
It's called the medial
prefrontal cortex

00:28:50.657 --> 00:28:52.710
but other brain regions as well.

00:28:52.710 --> 00:28:54.350
And then the final
element within

00:28:54.350 --> 00:28:55.963
the circuits
underlying creativity

00:28:55.963 --> 00:28:57.380
is the so-called
salience network.

00:28:57.380 --> 00:29:01.190
The salience network is a
network of brain regions

00:29:01.190 --> 00:29:03.980
that involves areas such as
the Insula, which actually

00:29:03.980 --> 00:29:05.750
has a complete map
of your body surface

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:07.960
as well as some
information mapped there

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:09.710
about what's going on
in the outside world

00:29:09.710 --> 00:29:10.940
and how those
combined with what's

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:12.648
going on in your
internal landscape, that

00:29:12.648 --> 00:29:13.700
is within your body.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.490
Also a brain region called
the ACC or intengu--

00:29:16.490 --> 00:29:20.185
excuse me, anterior cingulate
cortex and the amygdala.

00:29:20.185 --> 00:29:21.560
So a lot of
information is mapped

00:29:21.560 --> 00:29:24.207
within the salience
network about how we feel

00:29:24.207 --> 00:29:26.540
and how we feel in relation
to things that are happening

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:28.110
around us and within us.

00:29:28.110 --> 00:29:30.752
And the salience network
has one main job,

00:29:30.752 --> 00:29:32.210
which is to pay
attention to what's

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:37.070
most interesting either
in the world or inside us

00:29:37.070 --> 00:29:39.750
in terms of feelings
or experiences.

00:29:39.750 --> 00:29:42.530
So we've got three networks--
executive network, which

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:46.130
is there to suppress
choices in terms of actions

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.440
we could take but
decide not to, or things

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:51.320
we could think about but
choose not to or try not to,

00:29:51.320 --> 00:29:54.830
the default mode network,
which is basically the catalog

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:57.590
or library of
previous experiences

00:29:57.590 --> 00:30:01.760
that we have available to us
that would act as the paints

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:05.240
on a palette or the
possible ingredients

00:30:05.240 --> 00:30:06.590
that could go into a recipe.

00:30:06.590 --> 00:30:09.470
All of that has to, again,
arise from previous experience.

00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:11.510
We can't close our
eyes and suddenly

00:30:11.510 --> 00:30:14.510
be able to access all the
melodies that we've never

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:18.410
heard before or all our ideas
and concepts and knowledge

00:30:18.410 --> 00:30:20.720
about music if we don't
have musical understanding

00:30:20.720 --> 00:30:21.920
or visual understanding.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:24.230
So we're really
drawing up the library.

00:30:24.230 --> 00:30:26.660
And that library tends to
be rather disorganized.

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:27.950
It swirls around.

00:30:27.950 --> 00:30:30.860
It's not very structured
unless we're actively trying

00:30:30.860 --> 00:30:32.150
to think about something.

00:30:32.150 --> 00:30:34.310
And then we have the
salience network,

00:30:34.310 --> 00:30:37.400
which is the networks within
the brain that decide or make

00:30:37.400 --> 00:30:39.950
choices about what's most
interesting to pay attention to

00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:41.520
in a given moment.

00:30:41.520 --> 00:30:45.290
So those three networks work
together to create things.

00:30:45.290 --> 00:30:47.480
And when I say create
things, we again

00:30:47.480 --> 00:30:50.780
have to really
definition of creativity.

00:30:50.780 --> 00:30:55.460
Creativity is a rearrangement
of existing elements into novel

00:30:55.460 --> 00:30:58.370
combinations that reveal
something fundamental about how

00:30:58.370 --> 00:31:00.290
we or the world works.

00:31:00.290 --> 00:31:02.730
And, this is very important.

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:05.990
It tends to be things
that are useful.

00:31:05.990 --> 00:31:07.910
Now, they can merely be
useful because they're

00:31:07.910 --> 00:31:10.040
entertaining or
thrilling, they can also

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.110
have a particular utility
or use in the world

00:31:12.110 --> 00:31:14.390
like a piece of technology
that is actually

00:31:14.390 --> 00:31:17.030
useful like an app
or a smartphone

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:20.060
or a computer actually
has utility or a vehicle.

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:23.840
There are creative acts that
led to the formation of vehicles

00:31:23.840 --> 00:31:25.280
and computers, et cetera.

00:31:25.280 --> 00:31:28.460
But the point is that
just merely coming up

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:30.950
with novel combinations of
things like wings on a fish

00:31:30.950 --> 00:31:33.170
tank, that's not
creative or it's not

00:31:33.170 --> 00:31:37.710
creative in any meaningful way
because it's simply not useful.

00:31:37.710 --> 00:31:41.390
It doesn't reveal anything
fundamental new or purposeful.

00:31:41.390 --> 00:31:43.190
It doesn't allow
us to think about

00:31:43.190 --> 00:31:45.770
or interact with the world
or ourselves in novel ways.

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:50.690
Whereas things, people,
actions, and ideas

00:31:50.690 --> 00:31:53.408
that are truly creative
really change the way

00:31:53.408 --> 00:31:54.950
that we are able to
access the world.

00:31:54.950 --> 00:31:58.580
They act as portals to the
world and to ourselves.

