00:58:06.167 --> 00:58:08.750
Now, I realize that for some of
you listening to this episode,

00:58:08.750 --> 00:58:12.160
we are probably at the point
along the pathway of concept

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:14.950
and definition
and mechanism that

00:58:14.950 --> 00:58:18.020
leaves you in a place
of real wanting a tool.

00:58:18.020 --> 00:58:20.960
And so I promise that I'm
going to get into more tools,

00:58:20.960 --> 00:58:25.360
but to satisfy you and to
make sure that you do indeed

00:58:25.360 --> 00:58:26.860
understand that
there are tools that

00:58:26.860 --> 00:58:29.230
can emerge from the information
that you already now

00:58:29.230 --> 00:58:30.130
have in mind.

00:58:30.130 --> 00:58:32.230
I do want to share with
you one particular tool

00:58:32.230 --> 00:58:35.860
from the literature that
has been demonstrated over

00:58:35.860 --> 00:58:39.850
and over again to support
and build and enhance

00:58:39.850 --> 00:58:41.475
divergent thinking.

00:58:41.475 --> 00:58:42.850
And I also want
to share with you

00:58:42.850 --> 00:58:45.225
a tool that has been shown
from the scientific literature

00:58:45.225 --> 00:58:47.830
to enhance convergent thinking
because both convergent and

00:58:47.830 --> 00:58:50.600
divergent thinking are critical
for the creative process.

00:58:50.600 --> 00:58:55.630
Now, I should emphasize
that some people out there

00:58:55.630 --> 00:58:58.630
either by training or
by genetics or by both

00:58:58.630 --> 00:59:02.860
will be naturally better
at divergent or convergent

00:59:02.860 --> 00:59:03.550
thinking.

00:59:03.550 --> 00:59:08.560
And in fact, we now know
in almost poetic way

00:59:08.560 --> 00:59:11.380
that naturally
occurring variations

00:59:11.380 --> 00:59:15.010
in genes which underlie
naturally occurring variations

00:59:15.010 --> 00:59:20.230
in the percentage of
dopamine in one set of brain

00:59:20.230 --> 00:59:24.010
circuits versus another do,
seem to relate to whether or not

00:59:24.010 --> 00:59:26.320
people are naturally good
at divergent thinking

00:59:26.320 --> 00:59:27.640
or convergent thinking.

00:59:27.640 --> 00:59:31.143
Now, that's a very
nature based explanation

00:59:31.143 --> 00:59:33.310
for why some people are
better at divergent thinking

00:59:33.310 --> 00:59:36.720
and other people are better
at convergent thinking.

00:59:36.720 --> 00:59:39.150
Nature and nurture is
something that can never really

00:59:39.150 --> 00:59:42.150
be teased apart exactly because,
of course, if someone has

00:59:42.150 --> 00:59:44.550
a natural proclivity for
something based on their genes,

00:59:44.550 --> 00:59:48.180
you can't often separate
that from their parents

00:59:48.180 --> 00:59:51.000
because we inherit our
genes from our parents

00:59:51.000 --> 00:59:53.370
although even in cases
where people are raised away

00:59:53.370 --> 00:59:57.060
from their parents through
adoption, et cetera.

00:59:57.060 --> 00:59:59.172
It's very hard to separate
nature and nurture

00:59:59.172 --> 01:00:01.380
because somebody with a
natural proclivity for things

01:00:01.380 --> 01:00:04.110
might engage in those things
more, et cetera, et cetera.

01:00:04.110 --> 01:00:06.120
The point is that
for those of you that

01:00:06.120 --> 01:00:08.280
are very, very good
at divergent thinking

01:00:08.280 --> 01:00:11.070
or very, very good at convergent
thinking, some of that

01:00:11.070 --> 01:00:12.720
might have been inherited.

01:00:12.720 --> 01:00:16.050
But more than likely some of
that depended on the activities

01:00:16.050 --> 01:00:19.380
that you engaged in
in your early years,

01:00:19.380 --> 01:00:23.310
in particular in the years
between age 5 and 25.

01:00:23.310 --> 01:00:26.850
And for those of you that
are aged between 5 and 25,

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:29.280
all I can say is
please learn to engage

01:00:29.280 --> 01:00:31.477
both divergent and convergent
thinking as much as

01:00:31.477 --> 01:00:33.810
possible because you will
enhance your ability for both.

01:00:33.810 --> 01:00:35.760
For those of you
25 and older, you

01:00:35.760 --> 01:00:37.860
can still enhance
your ability to engage

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:39.390
divergent and
convergent thinking.

01:00:39.390 --> 01:00:43.350
And the fortunate news,
the equalizer I should say

01:00:43.350 --> 01:00:45.450
is that regardless
of whether or not

01:00:45.450 --> 01:00:47.760
you're a naturally better
at divergent or convergent

01:00:47.760 --> 01:00:50.160
thinking or you acquired
it through activities,

01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:52.330
you need both in
order to be creative.

01:00:52.330 --> 01:00:55.020
So what we know is that in order
to engage divergent thinking,

01:00:55.020 --> 01:00:56.940
we need access to
our memory banks.

01:00:56.940 --> 01:00:58.668
We need to come up
with possibilities.

01:00:58.668 --> 01:01:00.210
And those possibilities
can only come

01:01:00.210 --> 01:01:04.377
from what's contained within
our memory systems of our brain.

01:01:04.377 --> 01:01:05.960
Areas like the
hippocampus, et cetera.

01:01:05.960 --> 01:01:07.430
But the names
again don't matter.

01:01:07.430 --> 01:01:09.230
We just know that
if we are going

01:01:09.230 --> 01:01:11.990
to come up with novel
combinations of things or novel

01:01:11.990 --> 01:01:14.810
uses of things or totally
new ideas about how

01:01:14.810 --> 01:01:17.570
objects or notes of
music or foods or tastes

01:01:17.570 --> 01:01:20.030
or whatever can be
combined, we have

01:01:20.030 --> 01:01:21.690
to do that with
pre-existing knowledge.

01:01:21.690 --> 01:01:23.360
And yet what we
need to do in order

01:01:23.360 --> 01:01:26.780
to engage divergent
thinking is suppress

01:01:26.780 --> 01:01:30.140
what is called
autobiographical narratives

01:01:30.140 --> 01:01:32.330
and in particular,
autobiographical narratives.

01:01:32.330 --> 01:01:35.090
We need to discard
with judgments

01:01:35.090 --> 01:01:38.030
about how certain
combinations of things

01:01:38.030 --> 01:01:39.950
impacted us in the past.

01:01:39.950 --> 01:01:41.900
This here I think
is what people mean

01:01:41.900 --> 01:01:44.840
when they encourage the
exploration of creativity

01:01:44.840 --> 01:01:46.970
by so-called
boundary exploration.

01:01:46.970 --> 01:01:48.830
You hear about this a
lot in the self-help,

01:01:48.830 --> 01:01:50.030
in psychology literature.

01:01:50.030 --> 01:01:52.100
And I'm not at all
disparaging of that literature

01:01:52.100 --> 01:01:55.700
although rarely does it define
exactly how and why to go

01:01:55.700 --> 01:01:57.800
about being more
creative or in this case

01:01:57.800 --> 01:02:00.620
to be more divergent
in our thinking.

01:02:00.620 --> 01:02:02.750
So they'll say, you
have to take risks

01:02:02.750 --> 01:02:05.090
or you have to
suppress judgment.

01:02:05.090 --> 01:02:06.660
But how do you actually do that?

01:02:06.660 --> 01:02:09.800
Well, there's a wonderful
paper that talks about one way

01:02:09.800 --> 01:02:10.370
to do it.

01:02:10.370 --> 01:02:13.280
One way to do it is what's
called open monitoring

01:02:13.280 --> 01:02:16.118
meditation or even just
open monitoring thinking.

01:02:16.118 --> 01:02:17.660
And just to make
what could otherwise

01:02:17.660 --> 01:02:20.847
be a somewhat complex
section here very simple.

01:02:20.847 --> 01:02:22.430
What I'll also tell
you is that if you

01:02:22.430 --> 01:02:24.920
want to enhance
convergent thinking,

01:02:24.920 --> 01:02:27.350
you can do that
a number of ways,

01:02:27.350 --> 01:02:29.570
but you can do that
in particular by doing

01:02:29.570 --> 01:02:33.230
a different type of meditation
or thought process, which

01:02:33.230 --> 01:02:35.580
is called focused
attention meditation.

01:02:35.580 --> 01:02:37.680
So let's talk about open
monitoring meditation

01:02:37.680 --> 01:02:39.230
and why it's so
useful for enhancing

01:02:39.230 --> 01:02:41.000
divergent thinking,
this critical element

01:02:41.000 --> 01:02:43.620
of the creative process.

01:02:43.620 --> 01:02:45.800
First of all, open
monitoring meditation

01:02:45.800 --> 01:02:50.040
and focused attention
meditation can be performed

01:02:50.040 --> 01:02:52.100
the exact same way physically.

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:54.172
You can sit there,
eyes closed, I

01:02:54.172 --> 01:02:55.880
don't care if you're
in a Lotus position,

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:58.547
it doesn't really matter, you're
lying down, you're standing up.

01:02:58.547 --> 01:03:01.460
You could in theory do open
monitoring meditation with eyes

01:03:01.460 --> 01:03:03.870
open and that would be an
interesting variant on it.

01:03:03.870 --> 01:03:06.050
But for sake of the
discussion right

01:03:06.050 --> 01:03:07.520
now, let's just
focus on the study

01:03:07.520 --> 01:03:11.420
that talks about these
specific tools and the way

01:03:11.420 --> 01:03:13.320
that they were
used in the study.

01:03:13.320 --> 01:03:16.070
The title of the paper that
I'm essentially summarizing

01:03:16.070 --> 01:03:18.560
is called open monitoring
meditation reduces

01:03:18.560 --> 01:03:21.660
the involvement of brain regions
related to memory function.

01:03:21.660 --> 01:03:23.360
Now, right off the
bat that will cue you

01:03:23.360 --> 01:03:24.510
to something interesting.

01:03:24.510 --> 01:03:27.170
Something about divergent
thinking and open monitoring

01:03:27.170 --> 01:03:29.990
is related to
suppressing memory.

01:03:29.990 --> 01:03:32.390
But as you recall,
just a few moments ago,

01:03:32.390 --> 01:03:35.220
I said that in order to
engage in divergent thinking,

01:03:35.220 --> 01:03:39.350
you need to kill off
the narratives of what

01:03:39.350 --> 01:03:41.810
has to be related to what and
come up with new narratives.

01:03:41.810 --> 01:03:43.970
You still need to
understand possibilities

01:03:43.970 --> 01:03:47.110
but you need to forget
prior understanding of what

01:03:47.110 --> 01:03:49.610
those possibilities have to be
and start thinking about what

01:03:49.610 --> 01:03:51.300
those possibilities could be.

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:53.300
And so that it turns
out involves suppression

01:03:53.300 --> 01:03:55.430
of certain brain areas.

01:03:55.430 --> 01:03:57.620
Open monitoring
meditation is typically

01:03:57.620 --> 01:04:01.460
done for about 10 to 30 minutes
although it could be longer.

01:04:01.460 --> 01:04:03.950
And unlike other
forms of meditation

01:04:03.950 --> 01:04:06.590
where you sit and
concentrate on your breathing

01:04:06.590 --> 01:04:09.350
and trying to redirect your
thinking back to your breathing

01:04:09.350 --> 01:04:14.030
or to your posture or
to a chant or a mantra,

01:04:14.030 --> 01:04:16.790
open monitoring
meditation is simply

01:04:16.790 --> 01:04:19.940
a matter of having sit there
or lie down and close your eyes

01:04:19.940 --> 01:04:24.050
and to allow whatever surfaces
in your mind to surface.

01:04:24.050 --> 01:04:27.950
And what you practice is
the practice of nonjudgment.

01:04:27.950 --> 01:04:30.470
Now, nonjudgment itself is a
little bit of an abstract theme

01:04:30.470 --> 01:04:33.680
because, of course, the
moment you say don't judge you

01:04:33.680 --> 01:04:34.820
and others start to judge.

01:04:34.820 --> 01:04:36.020
It's just the way
that the brain works.

01:04:36.020 --> 01:04:37.490
You say, don't think
about an elephant

01:04:37.490 --> 01:04:38.270
you think about an elephant.

01:04:38.270 --> 01:04:39.395
That's a perfectly natural.

01:04:39.395 --> 01:04:42.823
You go to an edge of a bridge
or a cliff and you think about

01:04:42.823 --> 01:04:44.240
jumping off even
though you don't.

01:04:44.240 --> 01:04:45.343
Please don't jump off.

01:04:45.343 --> 01:04:47.510
And that's because it's
part of the circuitry that's

01:04:47.510 --> 01:04:48.680
keeping you from jumping off.

01:04:48.680 --> 01:04:50.390
Is the thought about what
would happen if you did.

01:04:50.390 --> 01:04:51.020
OK?

01:04:51.020 --> 01:04:53.510
So open monitoring
meditation involves

01:04:53.510 --> 01:04:56.270
dedicating a certain amount of
time where you close your eyes

01:04:56.270 --> 01:04:59.480
and whatever thoughts arise,
whatever emotions arise,

01:04:59.480 --> 01:05:03.380
whatever ideas arise
to watch those and take

01:05:03.380 --> 01:05:06.740
an inventory of them to just
merely watch them show up

01:05:06.740 --> 01:05:10.098
and pass or maybe become fixated
on them for some period of time

01:05:10.098 --> 01:05:12.140
or maybe even just one
for a long period of time.

01:05:12.140 --> 01:05:13.320
All of that is fine.

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:16.070
In other words, whatever
surfaces surfaces.

01:05:16.070 --> 01:05:17.840
That's open
monitoring meditation.

01:05:17.840 --> 01:05:20.300
And that we know from
brain imaging studies

01:05:20.300 --> 01:05:22.280
and we know from
measurements of dopamine,

01:05:22.280 --> 01:05:26.630
in particular brain circuits and
we know from people who train

01:05:26.630 --> 01:05:30.410
with open monitoring
meditation on a regular basis

01:05:30.410 --> 01:05:33.600
improves divergent
thinking capability.

01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:37.640
So in terms of tools, practicing
open monitoring meditation

01:05:37.640 --> 01:05:40.370
or what I would just call
open monitoring thinking

01:05:40.370 --> 01:05:42.530
is going to be immensely useful.

01:05:42.530 --> 01:05:44.150
And this is actually
an opportunity

01:05:44.150 --> 01:05:46.358
to queue up something that
I mentioned in our episode

01:05:46.358 --> 01:05:49.010
on meditation, which goes deep
into the different meditation

01:05:49.010 --> 01:05:51.122
involving focus inward
and outward, et cetera.

01:05:51.122 --> 01:05:52.830
You're welcome to
check out that episode.

01:05:52.830 --> 01:05:54.290
It's at hubermanlab.com.

01:05:54.290 --> 01:05:58.220
But the point is that rather
than think about the word

01:05:58.220 --> 01:06:02.030
meditation, which carries a
bunch of ideas about what it is

01:06:02.030 --> 01:06:04.270
and what it isn't
and how to do it,

01:06:04.270 --> 01:06:06.815
meditation is really just
a perceptual exercise.

01:06:06.815 --> 01:06:08.440
For instance, you
could do a meditation

01:06:08.440 --> 01:06:11.710
where you look at a single
point on a wall for five minutes

01:06:11.710 --> 01:06:14.332
and redirect your focus to that
single point on a wall over

01:06:14.332 --> 01:06:15.790
and over again
every time your mind

01:06:15.790 --> 01:06:17.380
drifts as it no doubt would.

01:06:17.380 --> 01:06:19.060
Or to a tone in the room.

01:06:19.060 --> 01:06:21.100
You could attend to that
and redirect to that.

01:06:21.100 --> 01:06:22.892
Rather than think about
it as a meditation,

01:06:22.892 --> 01:06:24.740
it's really just a
perceptual exercise.

01:06:24.740 --> 01:06:26.530
That's all that meditation is.

01:06:26.530 --> 01:06:28.720
So open monitoring
meditation is really

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:31.270
just a form of perception
where you're paying attention,

01:06:31.270 --> 01:06:34.510
you're perceiving your thoughts
without laying judgment

01:06:34.510 --> 01:06:36.670
to those thoughts or
trying not to lay judgment

01:06:36.670 --> 01:06:37.420
to those thoughts.

01:06:37.420 --> 01:06:38.795
And what people
find is that they

01:06:38.795 --> 01:06:40.780
very quickly within
a few days get better

01:06:40.780 --> 01:06:42.310
at doing open
monitoring meditation.

01:06:42.310 --> 01:06:45.040
And fortunately,
within just a few days

01:06:45.040 --> 01:06:48.400
and certainly within about a
week or more of practice and it

01:06:48.400 --> 01:06:50.310
doesn't even have to
be daily practice.

01:06:50.310 --> 01:06:52.000
So although, of
course, daily practice

01:06:52.000 --> 01:06:53.560
will accelerate the
process further,

01:06:53.560 --> 01:06:56.778
people become significantly
better at divergent thinking.

01:06:56.778 --> 01:06:58.570
And that's because of
the dopamine circuits

01:06:58.570 --> 01:07:01.450
and in particular, along
the nigrostriatal pathway

01:07:01.450 --> 01:07:02.500
becoming more active.

01:07:02.500 --> 01:07:04.120
And the wonderful
thing is that when

01:07:04.120 --> 01:07:07.210
you repeat a practice and
a particular neural circuit

01:07:07.210 --> 01:07:09.520
is engaged over and
over again deliberately,

01:07:09.520 --> 01:07:11.960
that neural circuit
becomes easier to engage.

01:07:11.960 --> 01:07:13.870
So-called neuroplasticity.

01:07:13.870 --> 01:07:16.900
So I would encourage
any of you that

01:07:16.900 --> 01:07:19.960
want to explore the creative
process for whatever reason

01:07:19.960 --> 01:07:21.520
or get better the
creative process,

01:07:21.520 --> 01:07:23.140
dedicate some amount of time.

01:07:23.140 --> 01:07:25.840
Maybe even just five
minutes every other day

01:07:25.840 --> 01:07:27.970
to doing this open
monitoring meditation.

01:07:27.970 --> 01:07:29.290
I've tried this meditation.

01:07:29.290 --> 01:07:33.470
It's actually quite fun to
do because at least to me

01:07:33.470 --> 01:07:37.330
it feels a lot easier than
the meditation associated

01:07:37.330 --> 01:07:38.710
with convergent thinking.

01:07:38.710 --> 01:07:41.050
Now, the convergent
thinking meditation

01:07:41.050 --> 01:07:43.550
is the so-called focus
attention meditation.

01:07:43.550 --> 01:07:45.490
And that's also described
in the same study.

01:07:45.490 --> 01:07:48.430
And other studies have
explored which particular brain

01:07:48.430 --> 01:07:49.750
networks it involves.

01:07:49.750 --> 01:07:52.060
And I can just tell
you that focused

01:07:52.060 --> 01:07:54.370
attention meditation,
which you can think of

01:07:54.370 --> 01:07:57.130
or I'd prefer that you think of
just as a perceptual exercise

01:07:57.130 --> 01:07:59.680
involves sitting or lying
down, closing your eyes,

01:07:59.680 --> 01:08:03.250
focusing either on your breath,
or some element of your body.

01:08:03.250 --> 01:08:07.480
Could be the tops of your knees
or the clasp of your hands.

01:08:07.480 --> 01:08:10.510
It could be focusing
on an auditory tone,

01:08:10.510 --> 01:08:14.230
you could even do it eyes open
and stare at a point on a wall

01:08:14.230 --> 01:08:15.460
or a flame of light.

01:08:15.460 --> 01:08:17.260
Whatever it happens
to be that allows

01:08:17.260 --> 01:08:20.859
you to redirect your focus to
a particular location or idea

01:08:20.859 --> 01:08:22.210
or sound.

01:08:22.210 --> 01:08:26.620
That is known to
improve your ability

01:08:26.620 --> 01:08:30.010
to engage convergent
thinking, to quickly

01:08:30.010 --> 01:08:34.330
parse through or analyze a
bunch of different choices,

01:08:34.330 --> 01:08:38.470
and to persist in
choice selection

01:08:38.470 --> 01:08:41.450
and therefore more rapidly
arrive at the correct answer.

01:08:41.450 --> 01:08:43.700
This is well-established
and, in fact, in, the episode

01:08:43.700 --> 01:08:45.832
I did with a wonderful
guest Dr. Wendy Suzuki

01:08:45.832 --> 01:08:47.290
from New York
University she talked

01:08:47.290 --> 01:08:50.770
about how a daily meditation
of about 10 to 13 minutes

01:08:50.770 --> 01:08:52.120
performed for about eight weeks.

01:08:52.120 --> 01:08:53.162
That's what they explore.

01:08:53.162 --> 01:08:55.359
And that study greatly
increases people's ability

01:08:55.359 --> 01:08:57.981
to focus and in
fact, their memory.

01:08:57.981 --> 01:08:59.439
And that's exactly
the point, which

01:08:59.439 --> 01:09:02.022
is that convergent thinking as
I mentioned before, it requires

01:09:02.022 --> 01:09:06.102
persistence, focus, and
access to specific memories.

01:09:06.102 --> 01:09:08.560
So if you are somebody who
wants to get better at focusing,

01:09:08.560 --> 01:09:10.210
that is the meditation for you.

01:09:10.210 --> 01:09:13.277
However, because today we're
talking about creativity,

01:09:13.277 --> 01:09:15.069
if you are somebody
who wants to get better

01:09:15.069 --> 01:09:17.200
at divergent thinking
and convergent thinking,

01:09:17.200 --> 01:09:18.760
the two elements of creativity.

01:09:18.760 --> 01:09:23.170
That is, I would encourage
you to do a dual meditation.

01:09:23.170 --> 01:09:25.270
That is, a meditation
that starts

01:09:25.270 --> 01:09:29.050
with open monitoring for
maybe 5 to 10 minutes

01:09:29.050 --> 01:09:33.460
and then transitions to
focused attention for maybe

01:09:33.460 --> 01:09:34.510
5 to 10 minutes.

01:09:34.510 --> 01:09:38.500
Because the positioning
of divergent thinking

01:09:38.500 --> 01:09:41.680
and then convergent thinking
close together, more closely

01:09:41.680 --> 01:09:44.439
resembles what the
creative process really is

01:09:44.439 --> 01:09:45.880
and what it typically involves.

01:09:45.880 --> 01:09:48.880
Most of us would love to
have a situation where

01:09:48.880 --> 01:09:52.660
we can spend a morning or a
day or a week brainstorming.

01:09:52.660 --> 01:09:53.620
Just brainstorming.

01:09:53.620 --> 01:09:55.117
Whatever we think about is fine.

01:09:55.117 --> 01:09:56.200
That's divergent thinking.

01:09:56.200 --> 01:09:58.660
Whatever elements just throw
them up on the whiteboard.

01:09:58.660 --> 01:10:00.310
We sometimes see
people and companies

01:10:00.310 --> 01:10:01.570
doing this at retreats.

01:10:01.570 --> 01:10:03.550
You bring people into
a novel environment.

01:10:03.550 --> 01:10:05.217
You say, let's just
forget all the rules

01:10:05.217 --> 01:10:08.670
and let's just come up with
new ideas about something.

01:10:08.670 --> 01:10:10.930
New uses of something,
new strategies.

01:10:10.930 --> 01:10:12.790
And nothing is too crazy.

01:10:12.790 --> 01:10:13.750
Nothing is off limits.

01:10:13.750 --> 01:10:15.700
And sure, that's
a useful exercise

01:10:15.700 --> 01:10:16.990
so-called brainstorming.

01:10:16.990 --> 01:10:19.240
But at some point,
there's the requirement

01:10:19.240 --> 01:10:20.143
to cross off things.

01:10:20.143 --> 01:10:22.060
And typically that's
done later in the retreat

01:10:22.060 --> 01:10:24.710
or later in the meeting
or later in the weekend.

01:10:24.710 --> 01:10:27.790
And that's a wonderful
way to approach creativity

01:10:27.790 --> 01:10:29.140
and to try and be creative.

01:10:29.140 --> 01:10:32.510
But not a lot of people train
for that on a regular basis.

01:10:32.510 --> 01:10:34.150
So what I just
described to you are

01:10:34.150 --> 01:10:36.002
research tested
tools for training

01:10:36.002 --> 01:10:37.960
for divergent thinking
and convergent thinking.

01:10:37.960 --> 01:10:39.460
And I would encourage
people who are

01:10:39.460 --> 01:10:42.490
interested in being more
creative to try and do these

01:10:42.490 --> 01:10:43.970
on a somewhat regular basis.

01:10:43.970 --> 01:10:47.560
If not every day then certainly
a few times a week or more.

01:10:47.560 --> 01:10:49.492
Certainly the more
you do it, the better

01:10:49.492 --> 01:10:50.450
you're going to get it.

01:10:50.450 --> 01:10:52.450
That's well demonstrated
in the literature.

01:10:52.450 --> 01:10:54.533
And if you're somebody
who's very consistent doing

01:10:54.533 --> 01:10:57.440
maybe five minutes of open
monitoring meditation and five

01:10:57.440 --> 01:10:59.770
minutes immediately
after of focus attention

01:10:59.770 --> 01:11:01.870
meditation daily, you
can expect that you

01:11:01.870 --> 01:11:05.520
will get very, very good
at these processes very,

01:11:05.520 --> 01:11:06.450
very quickly.

01:11:06.450 --> 01:11:08.750
Now, I'm not going to go
into a lengthy description

01:11:08.750 --> 01:11:12.620
of the different lines of
evidence that the corresponding

01:11:12.620 --> 01:11:15.110
areas of the brain are active
in each of these different .

01:11:15.110 --> 01:11:16.918
Meditation but
what I can tell you

01:11:16.918 --> 01:11:18.710
is that there have been
some beautiful what

01:11:18.710 --> 01:11:20.810
are called loss of
function studies

01:11:20.810 --> 01:11:24.620
where particular brain areas
are either depleted of dopamine

01:11:24.620 --> 01:11:26.298
or where dopamine
in some cases I

01:11:26.298 --> 01:11:28.340
guess what we would call
gain of function studies

01:11:28.340 --> 01:11:30.080
although not the gain
of function studies

01:11:30.080 --> 01:11:32.205
associated with virology
different gain of function

01:11:32.205 --> 01:11:34.860
studies where you enhance the
level of dopamine in the brain.

01:11:34.860 --> 01:11:38.300
What you find is that both
divergent and convergent

01:11:38.300 --> 01:11:42.350
thinking are enhanced
when levels of dopamine

01:11:42.350 --> 01:11:43.460
are elevated.

01:11:43.460 --> 01:11:46.050
Now, we're not necessarily
talking about pharmacology

01:11:46.050 --> 01:11:46.550
here.

01:11:46.550 --> 01:11:48.170
It turns out that
there are other ways

01:11:48.170 --> 01:11:50.270
to elevate dopamine
that make us better

01:11:50.270 --> 01:11:53.810
at divergent and convergent
thinking in particular,

01:11:53.810 --> 01:11:55.490
by using mood.

01:11:55.490 --> 01:11:59.090
And now I'd like to
talk about what mood you

01:11:59.090 --> 01:12:02.310
are in when you happen to
start a creative process,

01:12:02.310 --> 01:12:06.680
or try and do a training such
as open monitoring meditation

01:12:06.680 --> 01:12:07.970
or focused meditation.

01:12:07.970 --> 01:12:12.300
How your mood relates to your
level of dopamine at baseline.

01:12:12.300 --> 01:12:14.610
What we call your
tonic as it's called,

01:12:14.610 --> 01:12:18.440
meaning consistent or
ongoing level of dopamine,

01:12:18.440 --> 01:12:21.440
how that dictates whether or
not you are going to be better

01:12:21.440 --> 01:12:24.710
at one particular aspect of
the creative process or another

01:12:24.710 --> 01:12:27.890
and how you can enhance
your creativity in the very

01:12:27.890 --> 01:12:32.060
short term, very quickly using
tools that are known to trigger

01:12:32.060 --> 01:12:34.940
additional release of dopamine,
which in some cases is good

01:12:34.940 --> 01:12:37.430
and in some cases is
bad, I should mention.

01:12:37.430 --> 01:12:41.180
And in other words,
determine how

01:12:41.180 --> 01:12:43.790
you feel in one
moment should dictate

01:12:43.790 --> 01:12:47.270
what tool you should use in
order to become more creative.

01:12:47.270 --> 01:12:50.180
The relationship between
mood and creativity

01:12:50.180 --> 01:12:52.850
is a fascinating
one that is bridged

01:12:52.850 --> 01:12:56.840
by one main feature, which is
the amount of dopamine present

01:12:56.840 --> 01:12:58.850
in this nigrostriatal pathway.

01:12:58.850 --> 01:13:00.950
And there's a really
wonderful correlate

01:13:00.950 --> 01:13:03.410
or measure of the
amount of dopamine

01:13:03.410 --> 01:13:05.300
that's active in
that pathway that

01:13:05.300 --> 01:13:08.540
can be addressed noninvasively
in the laboratory.

01:13:08.540 --> 01:13:10.250
As I mentioned, the
nigrostriatal pathway

01:13:10.250 --> 01:13:14.338
is involved in movement
and in eye blinking which,

01:13:14.338 --> 01:13:15.380
of course, is a movement.

01:13:15.380 --> 01:13:17.090
It's not a movement of
the sort that we typically

01:13:17.090 --> 01:13:18.590
think of when we
think of movements,

01:13:18.590 --> 01:13:20.510
but nonetheless it
relies on dopamine levels

01:13:20.510 --> 01:13:21.410
in this pathway.

01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:24.290
And in fact, we can
state very confidently

01:13:24.290 --> 01:13:26.900
that when dopamine
levels are elevated,

01:13:26.900 --> 01:13:29.360
the blinking reflex
is more active.

01:13:29.360 --> 01:13:30.440
People just blink more.

01:13:30.440 --> 01:13:33.920
When dopamine levels are lower
or less active in this pathway,

01:13:33.920 --> 01:13:35.420
people tend to blink less.

01:13:35.420 --> 01:13:38.750
So blink frequency
is a common measure

01:13:38.750 --> 01:13:42.350
in studies of dopamine
within this pathway that

01:13:42.350 --> 01:13:44.090
relate to creativity.

01:13:44.090 --> 01:13:45.650
The work that I'm
about to describe

01:13:45.650 --> 01:13:47.510
is largely the
work of two authors

01:13:47.510 --> 01:13:50.540
who have done wonderful
work across several papers.

01:13:50.540 --> 01:13:51.935
Unfortunately,
for me their names

01:13:51.935 --> 01:13:53.060
are difficult to pronounce.

01:13:53.060 --> 01:13:54.810
So I apologize to them
and their relatives

01:13:54.810 --> 01:13:57.890
for what is sure to be
incorrect pronunciation.

01:13:57.890 --> 01:14:00.230
But the last names
of these authors

01:14:00.230 --> 01:14:02.765
are Chermahini and Hommel.

01:14:02.765 --> 01:14:03.890
They're in the Netherlands.

01:14:03.890 --> 01:14:07.370
So Chermahini and Hommel done
a number of different papers

01:14:07.370 --> 01:14:09.350
or studies rather
of the relationship

01:14:09.350 --> 01:14:11.780
between blinking,
mood, and creativity

01:14:11.780 --> 01:14:13.730
in particular,
divergent thinking.

01:14:13.730 --> 01:14:16.010
What they found is that
if people are blinking

01:14:16.010 --> 01:14:18.200
fairly often and they
measure their mood

01:14:18.200 --> 01:14:20.090
through subjective
tests and if they

01:14:20.090 --> 01:14:22.640
were to do brain imaging,
which other studies have done,

01:14:22.640 --> 01:14:24.140
they find is that
those people can

01:14:24.140 --> 01:14:27.560
engage in divergent
thinking very easily.

01:14:27.560 --> 01:14:29.990
In other words,
being in a good mood

01:14:29.990 --> 01:14:32.660
facilitates divergent thinking.

01:14:32.660 --> 01:14:35.150
Now, some of you might
immediately say, well, duh,

01:14:35.150 --> 01:14:36.680
if you're in a good
mood you can be

01:14:36.680 --> 01:14:38.990
more playful about the
exploration about what

01:14:38.990 --> 01:14:40.760
could happen with
these notes of music

01:14:40.760 --> 01:14:42.350
or these foods, et cetera.

01:14:42.350 --> 01:14:43.580
But it's not so obvious.

01:14:43.580 --> 01:14:46.770
Because it turns out that if
your dopamine levels are very,

01:14:46.770 --> 01:14:50.828
very high, and this can
be measured noninvasively

01:14:50.828 --> 01:14:52.370
through the frequency
of blinks or it

01:14:52.370 --> 01:14:54.860
can be measured more invasively
through brain imaging

01:14:54.860 --> 01:14:57.140
even through blood
draws or other methods

01:14:57.140 --> 01:14:58.040
to measure dopamine.

01:14:58.040 --> 01:15:01.370
If dopamine levels are very,
very high, what you observe

01:15:01.370 --> 01:15:04.370
is that divergent thinking
is actually very, very poor.

01:15:04.370 --> 01:15:08.060
Now, a naturally occurring
truly pathological example,

01:15:08.060 --> 01:15:10.490
this would be something
like manic bipolar disorder

01:15:10.490 --> 01:15:13.100
where somebody is in the
manic phase or somebody who

01:15:13.100 --> 01:15:16.580
has taken methamphetamine or
cocaine, what tends to happen

01:15:16.580 --> 01:15:19.730
is that they have lots and lots
of ideas, all of those ideas

01:15:19.730 --> 01:15:21.182
seem really exciting to them.

01:15:21.182 --> 01:15:23.390
But if you were to talk to
them for any given moment,

01:15:23.390 --> 01:15:27.380
they would be very fixated on
one particular tunnel of ideas.

01:15:27.380 --> 01:15:30.095
And by being fixated on one
particular tunnel of ideas

01:15:30.095 --> 01:15:32.720
like the idea that they're going
to run for president tomorrow,

01:15:32.720 --> 01:15:35.847
this is unfortunately
typical of people

01:15:35.847 --> 01:15:38.180
who have bipolar, which is
not to say that everybody who

01:15:38.180 --> 01:15:41.210
runs for president is bipolar
rather people who are bipolar

01:15:41.210 --> 01:15:43.158
often have these
delusions of grandeur

01:15:43.158 --> 01:15:45.200
that they're somehow going
to be president simply

01:15:45.200 --> 01:15:47.450
because they decided to and that
they were selected to do this,

01:15:47.450 --> 01:15:48.645
et cetera, et cetera.

01:15:48.645 --> 01:15:50.270
Ideas about themselves
and other people

01:15:50.270 --> 01:15:51.950
that are very constrained.

01:15:51.950 --> 01:15:54.120
In other words,
not very divergent.

01:15:54.120 --> 01:15:57.650
So divergent thinking is favored
by having elevated levels

01:15:57.650 --> 01:16:00.908
of dopamine but not too high.

01:16:00.908 --> 01:16:02.700
Well, that, of course,
creates a conundrum.

01:16:02.700 --> 01:16:04.710
How do you know how
much dopamine you need

01:16:04.710 --> 01:16:07.080
and how to achieve those
elevated levels of dopamine.

01:16:07.080 --> 01:16:09.330
Well, leaving aside
people who are suffering

01:16:09.330 --> 01:16:13.980
from a manic episode what
Chermahini and Hommel have

01:16:13.980 --> 01:16:18.180
discovered is that if
people are in a low mood,

01:16:18.180 --> 01:16:20.400
they're not feeling great,
maybe they're depressed

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:22.067
but they're just not
feeling that great.

01:16:22.067 --> 01:16:25.830
They feel on a scale of 1 to 10,
around a 2, or a 3, maybe a 4.

01:16:25.830 --> 01:16:27.390
The probability
that they will be

01:16:27.390 --> 01:16:30.240
able to engage effectively
in divergent thinking

01:16:30.240 --> 01:16:31.570
is quite low.

01:16:31.570 --> 01:16:34.590
However, the good news
is, they are typically

01:16:34.590 --> 01:16:38.130
very susceptible to
elevations in mood

01:16:38.130 --> 01:16:42.430
through observing or
hearing positive stories,

01:16:42.430 --> 01:16:44.310
listening to music
that they like,

01:16:44.310 --> 01:16:47.102
any kind of so-called
inspirational stimuli.

01:16:47.102 --> 01:16:48.060
Now, this is good news.

01:16:48.060 --> 01:16:49.435
What this means
is that if you're

01:16:49.435 --> 01:16:51.907
somebody who's not feeling
very motivated to engage

01:16:51.907 --> 01:16:54.240
in divergent thinking, you're
not feeling very creative,

01:16:54.240 --> 01:16:56.070
you're feeling a little low.

01:16:56.070 --> 01:16:58.410
The thing to do in
that case is actually

01:16:58.410 --> 01:17:00.390
to take external stimuli.

01:17:00.390 --> 01:17:03.810
Things that you like and start
interacting with those stimuli

01:17:03.810 --> 01:17:05.850
to get your mood elevated
and then to engage

01:17:05.850 --> 01:17:07.260
in divergent thinking.

01:17:07.260 --> 01:17:11.550
However, what Chermahini
and Hommel have also shown

01:17:11.550 --> 01:17:17.580
is that if people are
already in a very good mood,

01:17:17.580 --> 01:17:20.370
elevating dopamine
further is not conducive.

01:17:20.370 --> 01:17:23.340
And in fact, is detrimental
to divergent thinking.

01:17:23.340 --> 01:17:27.510
And in that case, they would
be better off for example,

01:17:27.510 --> 01:17:32.250
not engaging in any
activities or taking anything

01:17:32.250 --> 01:17:34.140
in the way of pharmacology
that would further

01:17:34.140 --> 01:17:35.610
increase their dopamine.

01:17:35.610 --> 01:17:38.940
And probably limiting the
amount of external stimuli

01:17:38.940 --> 01:17:41.490
that are coming in through
music and visual stimuli

01:17:41.490 --> 01:17:43.620
and really focusing
on divergent thinking

01:17:43.620 --> 01:17:45.850
and the creative
process immediately.

01:17:45.850 --> 01:17:47.100
Now, this is important.

01:17:47.100 --> 01:17:49.290
In an earlier episode
both on bipolar

01:17:49.290 --> 01:17:50.880
and on other forms
of depression,

01:17:50.880 --> 01:17:56.370
I talked about how rates
of bipolar manic episodes

01:17:56.370 --> 01:17:59.310
and dopamine levels
and creativity

01:17:59.310 --> 01:18:00.850
tend to be correlated.

01:18:00.850 --> 01:18:04.200
Now, unfortunately, rates of
suicide are 20 to 30 times

01:18:04.200 --> 01:18:06.700
higher in people who have
bipolar disorder as well.

01:18:06.700 --> 01:18:09.167
And so there's a whole dark
side to the bipolar disorder

01:18:09.167 --> 01:18:11.250
that makes it a very, very
dangerous and important

01:18:11.250 --> 01:18:12.990
disorder to treat.

01:18:12.990 --> 01:18:16.440
But for sake of the
discussion of creativity,

01:18:16.440 --> 01:18:18.510
what this means is
that we all need

01:18:18.510 --> 01:18:20.310
to develop some
intuitive sense as to

01:18:20.310 --> 01:18:23.040
whether or not our mood is--

01:18:23.040 --> 01:18:26.460
suppose we could spin this into
three categories is kind of--

01:18:26.460 --> 01:18:30.160
yes, happy, excited, positive
mood, and, of course,

01:18:30.160 --> 01:18:32.010
there are going to
be levels to that.

01:18:32.010 --> 01:18:36.100
Low like, mmmh, or
meh in the middle.

01:18:36.100 --> 01:18:38.820
So if you're in a
low mood or meh mood,

01:18:38.820 --> 01:18:41.490
by all means engage
in something probably

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:43.830
for about 5 to 30
minutes that elevates

01:18:43.830 --> 01:18:46.410
your mood before trying to
engage in divergent thinking.

01:18:46.410 --> 01:18:48.840
However, if you happen to be
in a pretty positive mood,

01:18:48.840 --> 01:18:51.660
even if you're not 10
out of 10 on mood, then

01:18:51.660 --> 01:18:55.080
bringing in additional
stimuli to increase

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:57.240
your levels of dopamine
will not help you

01:18:57.240 --> 01:19:00.220
and in fact can hurt the
divergent thinking process.

01:19:00.220 --> 01:19:02.430
So in that case, I
would also encourage

01:19:02.430 --> 01:19:03.930
you to think about
something that

01:19:03.930 --> 01:19:05.730
was discussed on a
previous episode, which

01:19:05.730 --> 01:19:07.860
is the particular
effects of caffeine.

01:19:07.860 --> 01:19:09.700
I'll get into caffeine
a little bit later.

01:19:09.700 --> 01:19:12.840
But just very briefly,
caffeine increases

01:19:12.840 --> 01:19:14.710
levels of dopamine receptors.

01:19:14.710 --> 01:19:16.082
So it's not the caffeine is bad.

01:19:16.082 --> 01:19:17.790
In fact, caffeine can
be neuroprotective,

01:19:17.790 --> 01:19:19.810
it can enhance
focus and so forth.

01:19:19.810 --> 01:19:22.740
But divergent thinking
is anti focus.

01:19:22.740 --> 01:19:24.240
It requires just
enough focus to be

01:19:24.240 --> 01:19:26.190
able to come up with new ideas.

01:19:26.190 --> 01:19:28.680
But you actually don't
want to be overly focused.

01:19:28.680 --> 01:19:31.058
Focus is more conducive
to convergent thinking.

01:19:31.058 --> 01:19:32.850
In fact, that's exactly
what the literature

01:19:32.850 --> 01:19:36.240
shows, is that caffeine because
its effects on epinephrine

01:19:36.240 --> 01:19:38.640
and related systems in
the brain like adenosine.

01:19:38.640 --> 01:19:40.260
But mainly because
of its effects

01:19:40.260 --> 01:19:43.260
on persistence and
focus is very conducive

01:19:43.260 --> 01:19:44.820
to convergent thinking.

01:19:44.820 --> 01:19:48.180
So if you're somebody who
wants to explore creativity

01:19:48.180 --> 01:19:49.830
and wants to get
better at creativity,

01:19:49.830 --> 01:19:51.510
you now know that
you need to engage

01:19:51.510 --> 01:19:55.890
in divergent thinking and then
afterwards convergent thinking.

01:19:55.890 --> 01:19:59.370
I would recommend not using
stimulants such as caffeine

01:19:59.370 --> 01:20:02.940
prior to divergent thinking
but rather use stimulants

01:20:02.940 --> 01:20:05.490
if you do want to use
stimulants such as caffeine

01:20:05.490 --> 01:20:07.590
prior to convergent thinking.

01:20:07.590 --> 01:20:10.470
And in fact, in formulating
the architecture

01:20:10.470 --> 01:20:12.750
of today's episode,
which took me

01:20:12.750 --> 01:20:15.360
many hours across many
different days I confess,

01:20:15.360 --> 01:20:17.100
I actually decided to try this.

01:20:17.100 --> 01:20:19.470
In trying to imagine the
different configurations

01:20:19.470 --> 01:20:21.667
and ways that this
information can be organized,

01:20:21.667 --> 01:20:23.250
I deliberately
abstained from caffeine

01:20:23.250 --> 01:20:24.750
during those bouts of work.

01:20:24.750 --> 01:20:27.660
And when structuring
everything according

01:20:27.660 --> 01:20:30.030
to the decisions I
had already made,

01:20:30.030 --> 01:20:33.070
I purposely ingested
caffeine prior to that.

01:20:33.070 --> 01:20:36.360
Now, of course, constructing
a podcast episode

01:20:36.360 --> 01:20:40.062
is not really the ultimate
example of a creative act

01:20:40.062 --> 01:20:42.270
because, of course, it's
taking existing information.

01:20:42.270 --> 01:20:43.562
It's arranging in a novel ways.

01:20:43.562 --> 01:20:45.930
But it doesn't necessarily
allow key concepts

01:20:45.930 --> 01:20:48.210
to pop out in the way
that, for instance,

01:20:48.210 --> 01:20:51.330
Banksy or a Rothko or
an Escher would pop out.

01:20:51.330 --> 01:20:53.850
I'm certainly not naive
in thinking that it does.

01:20:53.850 --> 01:20:57.403
But the principle of is
what's important here.

01:20:57.403 --> 01:20:58.570
You need divergent thinking.

01:20:58.570 --> 01:20:59.778
You need convergent thinking.

01:20:59.778 --> 01:21:02.190
You need some level
of elevated dopamine

01:21:02.190 --> 01:21:05.010
in order to engage in
divergent thinking.

01:21:05.010 --> 01:21:08.025
But not so high that it starts
to inhibit that process.

01:21:08.025 --> 01:21:09.900
Now, if you were to come
into the laboratory,

01:21:09.900 --> 01:21:12.500
this could be measured by
your frequency of blinking.

01:21:12.500 --> 01:21:14.250
For better or for
worse, we can't actually

01:21:14.250 --> 01:21:16.860
count the number of times that
we blink unless we're actively

01:21:16.860 --> 01:21:17.740
paying attention to it.

01:21:17.740 --> 01:21:19.380
So I don't recommend that you
pay attention to your blinking

01:21:19.380 --> 01:21:21.090
because that will take
you off course from all

01:21:21.090 --> 01:21:22.860
the other important
things of your life.

01:21:22.860 --> 01:21:24.420
And how many times
you're blinking

01:21:24.420 --> 01:21:27.720
is rarely an important thing
for you to pay attention to.

01:21:27.720 --> 01:21:31.140
You can however, learn to
calibrate your mood that is,

01:21:31.140 --> 01:21:34.930
to assess your mood whether
or not you're in low, medium,

01:21:34.930 --> 01:21:36.000
or high mood.

01:21:36.000 --> 01:21:38.670
No problem using
that broad binning.

01:21:38.670 --> 01:21:39.920
You could scale it on 1 to 10.

01:21:39.920 --> 01:21:41.462
And then decide
whether or not you're

01:21:41.462 --> 01:21:43.050
going to use some
dopamine elevating

01:21:43.050 --> 01:21:44.280
stimulus from the outside.

01:21:44.280 --> 01:21:47.657
Again, could be music, could be
exercise, is an excellent way

01:21:47.657 --> 01:21:48.490
to elevate dopamine.

01:21:48.490 --> 01:21:50.430
I'll talk about another
well-established one

01:21:50.430 --> 01:21:52.320
from the research
literature that

01:21:52.320 --> 01:21:57.990
is known to elevate dopamine by
65% in the particular pathway

01:21:57.990 --> 01:22:00.120
that's relevant for
divergent thinking

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:01.908
and to do that without
any pharmacology.

01:22:01.908 --> 01:22:03.450
I'll share that with
you in a moment.

01:22:03.450 --> 01:22:06.990
But you need to decide
for you in a given moment

01:22:06.990 --> 01:22:10.620
or in a given work attempt
at creativity what you need

01:22:10.620 --> 01:22:11.860
and apply accordingly.

01:22:11.860 --> 01:22:16.230
Because as Chermahini and Hommel
have shown, whether or not

01:22:16.230 --> 01:22:18.972
you are in a low mood,
medium mood, or high mood,

01:22:18.972 --> 01:22:20.430
really can determine
whether or not

01:22:20.430 --> 01:22:23.580
you'll be able to access
divergent thinking or not.

01:22:23.580 --> 01:22:26.430
Now, if you're somebody who
already has an idea in mind,

01:22:26.430 --> 01:22:28.260
you're very excited
about a creative idea

01:22:28.260 --> 01:22:30.820
and you want to hone it,
you want to shape it,

01:22:30.820 --> 01:22:32.260
you want to pressure test it.

01:22:32.260 --> 01:22:33.885
We'll talk a little
bit more about what

01:22:33.885 --> 01:22:37.690
that means in a three step
process in just a little bit.

01:22:37.690 --> 01:22:40.480
I would strongly
encourage you to look

01:22:40.480 --> 01:22:43.420
at that process is a very
linear process in which there

01:22:43.420 --> 01:22:45.380
are right and wrong answers.

01:22:45.380 --> 01:22:48.610
And there, the use of caffeine
at appropriate dosages

01:22:48.610 --> 01:22:50.830
and dosages for
caffeine that are safe

01:22:50.830 --> 01:22:52.330
and in fact,
performance enhancing

01:22:52.330 --> 01:22:54.205
were covered in the
episode on caffeine turns

01:22:54.205 --> 01:22:57.470
out it's 1 to 3 milligrams
per kilogram of body weight,

01:22:57.470 --> 01:22:58.270
by the way.

01:22:58.270 --> 01:23:01.210
And if you want to
leverage caffeine or maybe

01:23:01.210 --> 01:23:04.750
even other forms of
healthy legal stimulants,

01:23:04.750 --> 01:23:06.500
those are covered in
the caffeine episode.

01:23:06.500 --> 01:23:08.583
And I'll talk about a few
more a little bit later.

01:23:08.583 --> 01:23:10.203
So to summarize this
segment and also

01:23:10.203 --> 01:23:11.620
just to make a
more general point,

01:23:11.620 --> 01:23:13.090
I think it's very
useful for people

01:23:13.090 --> 01:23:16.660
to start to pay attention to
what their tonic level, that

01:23:16.660 --> 01:23:18.940
is their baseline
level of dopamine

01:23:18.940 --> 01:23:22.600
ought to be in this
nigrostriatal circuit

01:23:22.600 --> 01:23:23.560
and in other circuits.

01:23:23.560 --> 01:23:26.140
And to do that by learning
to assess one's mood

01:23:26.140 --> 01:23:29.020
and pay attention to what
mood they happen to be in.

01:23:29.020 --> 01:23:30.490
And then to leverage tools.

01:23:30.490 --> 01:23:32.405
Behavioral tools, maybe
pharmacologic tools

01:23:32.405 --> 01:23:34.030
provided they're safe
and they're legal

01:23:34.030 --> 01:23:36.640
in order to either
increase dopamine

01:23:36.640 --> 01:23:39.550
or to elect not to
increase dopamine in order

01:23:39.550 --> 01:23:41.195
to access the creative process.

01:23:41.195 --> 01:23:43.070
Now, I've mentioned
pharmacology a few times.

01:23:43.070 --> 01:23:45.320
And I'd like to talk about
that just a little bit more

01:23:45.320 --> 01:23:46.780
in the context of dopamine.

01:23:46.780 --> 01:23:50.890
First of all, there is
no supplement or drug

01:23:50.890 --> 01:23:55.330
that you or anyone else can
take that will selectively

01:23:55.330 --> 01:23:59.230
elevate dopamine in only
one of the four circuits

01:23:59.230 --> 01:24:00.730
that I described before.

01:24:00.730 --> 01:24:03.190
This is just the state of
the technology nowadays.

01:24:03.190 --> 01:24:05.140
If you take a pill
or even if you

01:24:05.140 --> 01:24:06.940
were to inject some
substance, again, I

01:24:06.940 --> 01:24:10.360
hope this would be legal
and safe, et cetera.

01:24:10.360 --> 01:24:13.840
Whatever mode of delivery,
there is no technology

01:24:13.840 --> 01:24:17.230
that exists at this time that
would allow you to selectively

01:24:17.230 --> 01:24:18.460
amplify dopamine.

01:24:18.460 --> 01:24:21.688
For instance, just in the
nigrostriatal pathway or just

01:24:21.688 --> 01:24:23.230
in the means of
mesocortical pathway.

01:24:23.230 --> 01:24:25.022
Again, the nigrostriatal
pathway associated

01:24:25.022 --> 01:24:27.370
with diversion thinking, the
music mesocortical pathway

01:24:27.370 --> 01:24:30.430
associated with cognitive
persistence and convergent

01:24:30.430 --> 01:24:32.120
thinking.

01:24:32.120 --> 01:24:34.790
If you were to amplify
dopamine levels, for instance,

01:24:34.790 --> 01:24:36.560
by taking the amino
acid precursor

01:24:36.560 --> 01:24:38.270
to dopamine
L-Tyrosine, something

01:24:38.270 --> 01:24:39.860
that I occasionally
do to enhance

01:24:39.860 --> 01:24:42.020
dopamine levels for
sake of work or energy,

01:24:42.020 --> 01:24:45.020
500 milligrams or 1,000
milligrams even of L-Tyrosine.

01:24:45.020 --> 01:24:47.630
Sometimes I'll combine that with
other things like Alpha-GPC.

01:24:47.630 --> 01:24:50.450
It's going to enhance
dopamine transmission

01:24:50.450 --> 01:24:53.480
in the nigrostriatal pathway,
the mesocortical pathway,

01:24:53.480 --> 01:24:55.130
but also in the
mesolimbic pathway,

01:24:55.130 --> 01:24:58.350
and also, for that matter in
the tuberoinfundibular pathway

01:24:58.350 --> 01:24:59.600
associated with the pituitary.

01:24:59.600 --> 01:25:03.110
There is no way to direct
dopamine activation

01:25:03.110 --> 01:25:05.190
to just one of those pathways.

01:25:05.190 --> 01:25:08.220
That's just a reflection
of the existing technology.

01:25:08.220 --> 01:25:12.140
Now, this is also true if
you rely on illicit drugs

01:25:12.140 --> 01:25:13.050
to increase dopamine.

01:25:13.050 --> 01:25:14.633
So if it's cocaine
or methamphetamine,

01:25:14.633 --> 01:25:18.380
those will greatly increase
dopamine but nonselectively

01:25:18.380 --> 01:25:20.540
across all those
different pathways.

01:25:20.540 --> 01:25:25.010
And likewise with any drugs that
inhibit or block or antagonize

01:25:25.010 --> 01:25:26.600
as it's called dopamine.

01:25:26.600 --> 01:25:29.780
This is why people who, for
instance, have schizophrenia

01:25:29.780 --> 01:25:33.350
and take drugs to suppress
auditory hallucinations, some

01:25:33.350 --> 01:25:37.450
of those drugs work because they
block the so-called D2 receptor

01:25:37.450 --> 01:25:38.450
of the dopamine pathway.

01:25:38.450 --> 01:25:40.760
D2 receptors are
present in all four

01:25:40.760 --> 01:25:42.680
of the dopaminergic
pathways in the brain.

01:25:42.680 --> 01:25:45.470
And oftentimes, those
drugs will in fact

01:25:45.470 --> 01:25:47.780
suppress psychotic symptoms,
auditory hallucinations,

01:25:47.780 --> 01:25:49.760
et cetera, because
they reduce dopamine.

01:25:49.760 --> 01:25:52.850
But those people oftentimes will
have problems with movement.

01:25:52.850 --> 01:25:55.910
They will express what's called
in the clinical literature

01:25:55.910 --> 01:25:57.710
tardive dyskinesia.

01:25:57.710 --> 01:25:59.900
Writhing of the
face and the body

01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:02.698
from suppression
of dopamine within

01:26:02.698 --> 01:26:05.240
the nigrostriatal pathway, which
is associated with movement.

01:26:05.240 --> 01:26:07.820
They will sometimes have
deficits in eye blinking.

01:26:07.820 --> 01:26:09.830
People with Parkinson's
who actually

01:26:09.830 --> 01:26:13.040
have selective deficits of
dopamine within the substantia

01:26:13.040 --> 01:26:13.730
nigra.

01:26:13.730 --> 01:26:16.130
Nigrostriatal, remember,
it's substantia nigra

01:26:16.130 --> 01:26:17.435
show deficits in what?

01:26:17.435 --> 01:26:18.180
In movement.

01:26:18.180 --> 01:26:19.430
In the smoothness of movement.

01:26:19.430 --> 01:26:21.290
Oftentimes they
won't blink at all.

01:26:21.290 --> 01:26:22.820
They'll have a blank stare.

01:26:22.820 --> 01:26:24.690
And they have other
issues as well.

01:26:24.690 --> 01:26:27.830
So if you're somebody who
is interested in increasing

01:26:27.830 --> 01:26:31.430
dopamine through the use
of legal safe pharmacology

01:26:31.430 --> 01:26:33.930
as I would hope it
would be the case,

01:26:33.930 --> 01:26:35.780
there are ways to
do that, reasonably

01:26:35.780 --> 01:26:36.740
safely for most people.

01:26:36.740 --> 01:26:38.660
Again, people with
bipolar disorder issues,

01:26:38.660 --> 01:26:40.850
with the dopaminergic
pathway should not do this.

01:26:40.850 --> 01:26:44.390
I know nowadays there's
a lot of use of drugs

01:26:44.390 --> 01:26:50.510
that increase dopamine such as
Ritalin, Adderall, modafinil,

01:26:50.510 --> 01:26:53.630
armodafinil, often prescribed
for things like attention

01:26:53.630 --> 01:26:54.980
deficit hyperactivity disorder.

01:26:54.980 --> 01:26:57.860
We did an entire episode
on ADHD and pharmacologic,

01:26:57.860 --> 01:27:00.770
prescription, supplement, and
behavioral, nutritional tools

01:27:00.770 --> 01:27:01.400
for ADHD.

01:27:01.400 --> 01:27:03.770
You can find that episode
at hubermanlab.com.

01:27:03.770 --> 01:27:06.590
I know a number of people
take those compounds in order

01:27:06.590 --> 01:27:11.030
to increase dopamine and
focus for sake of studying

01:27:11.030 --> 01:27:14.120
or other activities staying
up long hours, et cetera.

01:27:14.120 --> 01:27:15.950
And the fact that
they increase focus,

01:27:15.950 --> 01:27:18.860
they are effective although
they do have their side effects.

01:27:18.860 --> 01:27:21.470
Sometimes severe, sometimes
habit forming, sometimes

01:27:21.470 --> 01:27:22.898
even addicting as well.

01:27:22.898 --> 01:27:24.440
But the fact that
they increase focus

01:27:24.440 --> 01:27:26.270
should automatically
tell you something

01:27:26.270 --> 01:27:29.540
that those drugs in
particular increase

01:27:29.540 --> 01:27:33.860
dopamine in the
so-called mesocortical

01:27:33.860 --> 01:27:35.750
and mesolimbic pathways.

01:27:35.750 --> 01:27:37.128
Why can I say that?

01:27:37.128 --> 01:27:39.170
How can I say that with
any degree of confidence?

01:27:39.170 --> 01:27:41.330
Well, there are these four
pathways ones involved

01:27:41.330 --> 01:27:43.730
in movement, but
these other ones

01:27:43.730 --> 01:27:46.850
are involved in motivation
and desire and reward.

01:27:46.850 --> 01:27:48.350
And I told you that
these things can

01:27:48.350 --> 01:27:50.210
be habit forming and
addicting in some cases

01:27:50.210 --> 01:27:52.910
and they are can
greatly increase focus.

01:27:52.910 --> 01:27:56.780
And focus is supported by
enhanced levels of dopamine

01:27:56.780 --> 01:28:00.660
within this mesolimbic
and mesocortical pathway.

01:28:00.660 --> 01:28:03.500
So yes, those drugs increase
dopamine across the board,

01:28:03.500 --> 01:28:05.960
but there does seem to be
some weighting of dopamine

01:28:05.960 --> 01:28:08.660
toward the systems involved
in motivation and reward

01:28:08.660 --> 01:28:11.270
and sometimes even leading to
habit formation and addiction.

01:28:11.270 --> 01:28:13.670
That's why those
drugs should only

01:28:13.670 --> 01:28:17.480
be taken with the close
supervision of a very

01:28:17.480 --> 01:28:20.750
skilled psychiatrist or somebody
else who's board certified

01:28:20.750 --> 01:28:23.330
who can really govern that.

01:28:23.330 --> 01:28:26.030
There are however, ways
to increase dopamine

01:28:26.030 --> 01:28:28.770
more evenly across the
board using nonprescription

01:28:28.770 --> 01:28:29.270
approaches.

01:28:29.270 --> 01:28:30.740
And one I already
mentioned, which

01:28:30.740 --> 01:28:33.440
is L-Tyrosine taken
typically in dosages

01:28:33.440 --> 01:28:35.120
of 500 to 1,000 milligrams.

01:28:35.120 --> 01:28:38.180
L-Tyrosine is not as potent
in increasing dopamine

01:28:38.180 --> 01:28:42.500
as are the prescription drugs
that I referred to before.

01:28:42.500 --> 01:28:43.490
Tends to be milder.

01:28:43.490 --> 01:28:45.950
For some people it can have
a very amplified effect.

01:28:45.950 --> 01:28:47.010
They feel it right away.

01:28:47.010 --> 01:28:50.420
It's very intense in
elevating focus and motivation

01:28:50.420 --> 01:28:52.400
and the desire to move.

01:28:52.400 --> 01:28:55.053
For other people,
it's less potent.

01:28:55.053 --> 01:28:56.720
It really depends on
a number of things.

01:28:56.720 --> 01:28:59.540
I should mention that regular
consumption of caffeine

01:28:59.540 --> 01:29:02.540
of 1 to 3 milligrams per
kilogram of body weight per day

01:29:02.540 --> 01:29:06.780
also will increase dopamine
receptor efficacy and density,

01:29:06.780 --> 01:29:09.950
which will make any existing
dopamine more effective

01:29:09.950 --> 01:29:12.260
whether or not that dopamine
is triggered by things

01:29:12.260 --> 01:29:14.630
like L-Tyrosine, or if
you're not taking anything

01:29:14.630 --> 01:29:15.720
to elevate dopamine.

01:29:15.720 --> 01:29:17.210
The dopamine that
you do make will

01:29:17.210 --> 01:29:20.570
be more effective in elevating
your mood, motivation,

01:29:20.570 --> 01:29:22.520
and desire to move.

01:29:22.520 --> 01:29:24.950
And by extension
divergent thinking.

01:29:24.950 --> 01:29:27.440
If you are consuming
caffeine, but, again, caffeine

01:29:27.440 --> 01:29:29.690
should be taken prior to
convergent thinking type task

01:29:29.690 --> 01:29:31.770
probably more than it
should be taken prior

01:29:31.770 --> 01:29:33.300
to divergent thinking task.

01:29:33.300 --> 01:29:35.670
And, of course, there are
other legal supplements

01:29:35.670 --> 01:29:37.080
that can elevate
dopamine as well

01:29:37.080 --> 01:29:39.960
and particular phenethylamine
is very effective in doing that.

01:29:39.960 --> 01:29:43.440
600 milligrams of that
has a brief effect lasting

01:29:43.440 --> 01:29:44.982
only about 30 to 45 minutes.

01:29:44.982 --> 01:29:46.440
But it is one that
many people find

01:29:46.440 --> 01:29:49.440
beneficial for sake of studying
or for creative thinking

01:29:49.440 --> 01:29:50.650
and so on and so forth.

01:29:50.650 --> 01:29:51.930
Now, that's pharmacology.

01:29:51.930 --> 01:29:54.028
And in fact, there's
an extensive landscape

01:29:54.028 --> 01:29:56.070
of prescription and
supplement based pharmacology

01:29:56.070 --> 01:29:57.900
and indeed nutrition.

01:29:57.900 --> 01:29:59.760
For instance, the
consumption of foods

01:29:59.760 --> 01:30:03.153
that are high in L-Tyrosine
such as aged Parmesan cheese,

01:30:03.153 --> 01:30:04.320
for instance, of all things.

01:30:04.320 --> 01:30:05.760
Very, very high in L-Tyrosine.

01:30:05.760 --> 01:30:07.200
The precursor to dopamine.

01:30:07.200 --> 01:30:08.100
Certain foods.

01:30:08.100 --> 01:30:09.570
You can look up
online which foods

01:30:09.570 --> 01:30:11.340
contain high levels
of L-Tyrosine

01:30:11.340 --> 01:30:13.590
and which ones are compatible
with your nutrition.

01:30:13.590 --> 01:30:16.980
But leaving pharmacology
aside, there's

01:30:16.980 --> 01:30:20.490
a very exciting
nonpharmacological tool.

01:30:20.490 --> 01:30:24.000
A purely behavioral tool
that the research literature

01:30:24.000 --> 01:30:27.870
has told us can selectively
increase dopamine

01:30:27.870 --> 01:30:30.750
within the
nigrostriatal pathway.

01:30:30.750 --> 01:30:33.120
The pathway that's involved
in divergent thinking.

01:30:33.120 --> 01:30:37.980
And can do so very dramatically
as much as 65% above baseline.

01:30:37.980 --> 01:30:40.560
And so this is a
behavioral tool that

01:30:40.560 --> 01:30:43.470
is useful for a number of things
but that I find particularly

01:30:43.470 --> 01:30:46.620
interesting in leveraging
towards the exploration

01:30:46.620 --> 01:30:48.850
and enhancement of creativity
because first of all,

01:30:48.850 --> 01:30:49.860
it's purely behavioral.

01:30:49.860 --> 01:30:51.630
So it's 0 cost.

01:30:51.630 --> 01:30:56.700
And it involves no manipulation
of brain neuromodulators

01:30:56.700 --> 01:30:58.380
or chemistry through
pharmacology.

01:30:58.380 --> 01:31:01.170
So it's something that you
can explore very safely

01:31:01.170 --> 01:31:03.210
and certainly not having
to purchase anything.

01:31:03.210 --> 01:31:06.570
And what's really remarkable
is the selectivity

01:31:06.570 --> 01:31:09.420
or I think it's fair to
say the immense selectivity

01:31:09.420 --> 01:31:12.300
that this particular
behavioral intervention seems

01:31:12.300 --> 01:31:15.900
to exert on dopamine within
this pathway associated

01:31:15.900 --> 01:31:17.100
with divergent thinking.

01:31:17.100 --> 01:31:18.960
So the study that
I'm about to describe

01:31:18.960 --> 01:31:21.425
is a study that
dates back 20 years.

01:31:21.425 --> 01:31:22.800
Now, that should
not concern you.

01:31:22.800 --> 01:31:27.150
In fact, the early arrival
of this study or what

01:31:27.150 --> 01:31:28.500
now seems to be early arrival.

01:31:28.500 --> 01:31:31.890
I mean, it wasn't that long
ago, is really exciting

01:31:31.890 --> 01:31:33.780
because the first
line of this study

01:31:33.780 --> 01:31:37.860
really illustrates how important
or how much of a landmark study

01:31:37.860 --> 01:31:38.530
this really is.

01:31:38.530 --> 01:31:40.350
And so I'll just read you
the first line of the study,

01:31:40.350 --> 01:31:41.975
then I'll tell you
the title, then I'll

01:31:41.975 --> 01:31:44.965
tell you what they discovered
in fairly top contour

01:31:44.965 --> 01:31:46.590
and we will provide
a link to the study

01:31:46.590 --> 01:31:49.060
if you want to peruse
it in more detail.

01:31:49.060 --> 01:31:52.230
The first line of the study
is this is the first in vivo,

01:31:52.230 --> 01:31:53.730
just meaning in the organism.

01:31:53.730 --> 01:31:56.050
In this case, this
was a study on humans.

01:31:56.050 --> 01:31:58.170
This is the first in
vivo demonstration

01:31:58.170 --> 01:32:01.680
of an association between an
endogenous neurotransmitter

01:32:01.680 --> 01:32:05.677
release, endogenous means within
us, and conscious experience.

01:32:05.677 --> 01:32:07.260
So what this sentence
essentially says

01:32:07.260 --> 01:32:09.840
is this is the first study
exploring how a chemical that's

01:32:09.840 --> 01:32:14.070
naturally released in our body
relates to a particular quality

01:32:14.070 --> 01:32:16.020
of conscious experience.

01:32:16.020 --> 01:32:18.660
This study was performed
in Scandinavia,

01:32:18.660 --> 01:32:20.853
in one of the
hospitals in Denmark.

01:32:20.853 --> 01:32:22.020
Again, we'll provide a link.

01:32:22.020 --> 01:32:23.443
The first author is Kjaer.

01:32:23.443 --> 01:32:25.860
I think I'm pronouncing it
correctly although probably not

01:32:25.860 --> 01:32:29.100
K-J-A-E-R et al.

01:32:29.100 --> 01:32:30.990
And the title of the
study is Increased

01:32:30.990 --> 01:32:32.940
Dopamine Tone During
Meditation Induced

01:32:32.940 --> 01:32:34.152
Change of Consciousness.

01:32:34.152 --> 01:32:36.360
And I want to just highlight
that the meditation used

01:32:36.360 --> 01:32:38.650
in this study isn't really
a meditation at all.

01:32:38.650 --> 01:32:40.830
I don't know why they
selected that for the title.

01:32:40.830 --> 01:32:43.110
The behavioral protocol
used in the study

01:32:43.110 --> 01:32:46.680
was more akin to what is
normally called yoga nidra

01:32:46.680 --> 01:32:49.510
or NSDR nonsleep depressed.

01:32:49.510 --> 01:32:52.020
Now yoga nidra and NSDR have
been discussed many times

01:32:52.020 --> 01:32:53.520
before on this podcast.

01:32:53.520 --> 01:32:55.350
Yoga nidra, for
instance, is a practice

01:32:55.350 --> 01:32:57.725
that's been around for hundreds
if not thousands of years

01:32:57.725 --> 01:33:00.150
in which people
deliberately lie still.

01:33:00.150 --> 01:33:03.420
So they're forcing themselves
to be mostly motionless,

01:33:03.420 --> 01:33:05.190
small movements are fine.

01:33:05.190 --> 01:33:06.780
And they're directing
their attention

01:33:06.780 --> 01:33:08.010
to the surface of
their body, they're

01:33:08.010 --> 01:33:09.210
doing long exhale breathing.

01:33:09.210 --> 01:33:12.510
Sometimes some intentions,
sometimes some visualization.

01:33:12.510 --> 01:33:15.060
But it's really
self-directed relaxation.

01:33:15.060 --> 01:33:18.960
And the key component is that
people stay awake and engage

01:33:18.960 --> 01:33:20.910
in very little movement.

01:33:20.910 --> 01:33:22.740
And the key word
there is movement.

01:33:22.740 --> 01:33:27.600
Now, nonsleep depressed is a
acronym, a term that I coined.

01:33:27.600 --> 01:33:29.640
It's not a term that
I coined in order

01:33:29.640 --> 01:33:32.760
to try and wipe away or
discard with yoga nidra.

01:33:32.760 --> 01:33:35.700
I'm a person who has
great respect for yoga

01:33:35.700 --> 01:33:36.990
nidra and its traditions.

01:33:36.990 --> 01:33:39.630
It's a term that
I coined in order

01:33:39.630 --> 01:33:42.660
to encompass a number of
practices that don't include

01:33:42.660 --> 01:33:46.073
any mystical type language
or scientific language

01:33:46.073 --> 01:33:46.740
for that matter.

01:33:46.740 --> 01:33:48.600
And that doesn't
involve intentions.

01:33:48.600 --> 01:33:52.290
It involves deep relaxation
yet remaining wide awake

01:33:52.290 --> 01:33:52.950
and conscious.

01:33:52.950 --> 01:33:54.870
Sometimes people fall
asleep and that's OK.

01:33:54.870 --> 01:33:57.510
But this is really an
atypical brain state

01:33:57.510 --> 01:34:03.480
of being deeply relaxed yet in
general awake and motionless.

01:34:03.480 --> 01:34:05.550
Again, motionless being the key.

01:34:05.550 --> 01:34:09.540
Very few brain states involve us
being mostly if not completely

01:34:09.540 --> 01:34:10.660
motionless and yet awake.

01:34:10.660 --> 01:34:12.660
And it turns out that
brain state whether or not

01:34:12.660 --> 01:34:14.820
you call it yoga nidra,
you call it NSDR,

01:34:14.820 --> 01:34:17.580
whether or not you call it
meditation induced shift

01:34:17.580 --> 01:34:20.320
in consciousness as
they did in this study,

01:34:20.320 --> 01:34:22.230
although they do
refer to yoga nidra,

01:34:22.230 --> 01:34:25.620
all refer to the same thing,
which is being motionless

01:34:25.620 --> 01:34:28.710
and yet aware and relaxed.

01:34:28.710 --> 01:34:29.550
I should mention.

01:34:29.550 --> 01:34:31.140
So in this study,
what they did was

01:34:31.140 --> 01:34:33.960
they subjects into
the laboratory.

01:34:33.960 --> 01:34:38.280
They had them either undergo
this self-directed deep

01:34:38.280 --> 01:34:41.700
relaxation while they are
motionless or mostly motionless

01:34:41.700 --> 01:34:44.970
or they had them listen
to an audio script

01:34:44.970 --> 01:34:47.280
while also just lying
there with eyes closed.

01:34:47.280 --> 01:34:49.448
And then they used a
number of chemical tricks.

01:34:49.448 --> 01:34:51.240
And I don't want to
get too deep into those

01:34:51.240 --> 01:34:52.770
now because they can be
a little bit distracting.

01:34:52.770 --> 01:34:53.860
For those of you
that are interested,

01:34:53.860 --> 01:34:55.193
you can look at it in the study.

01:34:55.193 --> 01:34:57.287
This is a binding of a
chemical in the brain

01:34:57.287 --> 01:34:59.370
that then they can image
with brain imaging, which

01:34:59.370 --> 01:35:03.060
is what they did in the study
to evaluate how much dopamine

01:35:03.060 --> 01:35:05.130
changed in the brain
and where specifically

01:35:05.130 --> 01:35:08.440
in the brain dopamine
changed its levels before,

01:35:08.440 --> 01:35:11.910
during, and after this
particular behavioral practice

01:35:11.910 --> 01:35:13.200
in one or the other group.

01:35:13.200 --> 01:35:16.020
And what they discovered
is that people

01:35:16.020 --> 01:35:18.210
who did this deep
relaxation, that

01:35:18.210 --> 01:35:22.050
is, self-directed deep
relaxation lying their eyes

01:35:22.050 --> 01:35:27.240
closed, relatively motionless
although small movements

01:35:27.240 --> 01:35:30.570
of the body or movements of
the head are absolutely fine.

01:35:30.570 --> 01:35:35.490
What they observed was a 65%
increase in dopamine release.

01:35:35.490 --> 01:35:36.270
Now, here its key.

01:35:36.270 --> 01:35:38.070
Dopamine release.

01:35:38.070 --> 01:35:41.490
And they observed an increase
in so-called theta activity.

01:35:41.490 --> 01:35:45.210
Theta activity is a pattern
of brain wave activity that's

01:35:45.210 --> 01:35:47.850
commonly associated
with creative states

01:35:47.850 --> 01:35:49.420
and divergent thinking
in particular.

01:35:49.420 --> 01:35:51.250
So that's important.

01:35:51.250 --> 01:35:53.790
And they observe
that across subjects

01:35:53.790 --> 01:35:56.140
specifically in the
nigrostriatal pathway.

01:35:56.140 --> 01:35:58.420
This pathway associated
with divergent thinking.

01:35:58.420 --> 01:35:59.693
So this is very exciting.

01:35:59.693 --> 01:36:01.110
This is a study
that really points

01:36:01.110 --> 01:36:04.680
to a behavioral tool that can
be used to selectively elevate

01:36:04.680 --> 01:36:08.520
dopamine in the very pathway
that one would want to if they

01:36:08.520 --> 01:36:10.440
wanted to engage divergent
thinking for sake

01:36:10.440 --> 01:36:12.180
of creative exploration.

01:36:12.180 --> 01:36:15.660
There are also a number of key
observations within this study.

01:36:15.660 --> 01:36:21.190
First of all, the reduction in
bodily movement was essential.

01:36:21.190 --> 01:36:26.460
In fact, when people rated or
when the amount of readiness

01:36:26.460 --> 01:36:30.330
for action in their system,
their body was evaluated.

01:36:30.330 --> 01:36:33.360
What people found was that
immediately after this practice

01:36:33.360 --> 01:36:35.040
they felt very still.

01:36:35.040 --> 01:36:37.833
In other words, they felt as
if remaining still was natural.

01:36:37.833 --> 01:36:39.750
Now it's not the case
that they couldn't move.

01:36:39.750 --> 01:36:41.190
In fact, the
elevation in dopamine

01:36:41.190 --> 01:36:44.880
that occurred during
this practice, this yoga

01:36:44.880 --> 01:36:48.960
nidra-like nonsleep or
NSDR-like practice, actually

01:36:48.960 --> 01:36:53.580
prepared them to be able to
move in a much more dedicated

01:36:53.580 --> 01:36:54.845
and robust way afterwards.

01:36:54.845 --> 01:36:56.970
But during the practice,
their readiness for action

01:36:56.970 --> 01:36:57.762
went way, way down.

01:36:57.762 --> 01:36:58.500
Not surprising.

01:36:58.500 --> 01:37:00.210
They were pretty
much motionless.

01:37:00.210 --> 01:37:05.160
But interestingly, as the
level of readiness for movement

01:37:05.160 --> 01:37:06.840
went down, down,
down, down down,

01:37:06.840 --> 01:37:09.510
their degree of
visual imagery, that

01:37:09.510 --> 01:37:12.390
is, their internal
landscape and their ability

01:37:12.390 --> 01:37:15.570
to imagine new things increased.

01:37:15.570 --> 01:37:17.640
And in fact, areas
of the brain that

01:37:17.640 --> 01:37:19.140
are associated
with visual imagery

01:37:19.140 --> 01:37:21.720
such as the visual or
so-called occipital

01:37:21.720 --> 01:37:24.960
cortex and the parietal cortex
has been shown in other studies

01:37:24.960 --> 01:37:26.973
to ramp up when
people are motionless.

01:37:26.973 --> 01:37:28.890
So there seems to be
this inverse relationship

01:37:28.890 --> 01:37:31.597
between movement and visual
imagery which makes sense.

01:37:31.597 --> 01:37:33.180
When we're moving
we can pay attention

01:37:33.180 --> 01:37:34.890
to things in the
outside world, we

01:37:34.890 --> 01:37:38.130
tend to be aware of our sensory
environment to varying degrees.

01:37:38.130 --> 01:37:40.800
But we don't tend to be very
focused on visual imagery

01:37:40.800 --> 01:37:43.740
within our head whereas when we
lie down or sit down and close

01:37:43.740 --> 01:37:46.350
our eyes and we are motionless,
the degree of visual imagery

01:37:46.350 --> 01:37:48.030
really increases.

01:37:48.030 --> 01:37:50.520
Hence, the increase
in divergent thinking

01:37:50.520 --> 01:37:52.110
because what
essentially is happening

01:37:52.110 --> 01:37:55.290
is the library of
options, the library

01:37:55.290 --> 01:37:57.130
of possible interactions
with whatever it

01:37:57.130 --> 01:37:58.380
is that you're thinking about.

01:37:58.380 --> 01:38:00.588
I gave the example, which
is a trivial one on purpose

01:38:00.588 --> 01:38:01.230
of a pen.

01:38:01.230 --> 01:38:04.830
But the bank of options
that becomes available

01:38:04.830 --> 01:38:06.960
when we are motionless
and when we are limiting

01:38:06.960 --> 01:38:10.200
our visualization of
the external world

01:38:10.200 --> 01:38:12.295
increases exponentially.

01:38:12.295 --> 01:38:13.170
So this is important.

01:38:13.170 --> 01:38:14.580
And what it points
to is the fact

01:38:14.580 --> 01:38:18.030
that this very simple completely
nonpharmacologic behavioral

01:38:18.030 --> 01:38:20.000
practice of lying
down motionless

01:38:20.000 --> 01:38:21.000
for some period of time.

01:38:21.000 --> 01:38:22.920
And I confess the amount
of time that they use

01:38:22.920 --> 01:38:24.910
in the study was quite long.

01:38:24.910 --> 01:38:26.940
It was longer than 60 minutes.

01:38:26.940 --> 01:38:30.870
But all the data that I'm aware
of in terms of NSDR and yoga

01:38:30.870 --> 01:38:32.850
nidra, and there's a
growing body of literature

01:38:32.850 --> 01:38:35.010
on these practices
I should mention,

01:38:35.010 --> 01:38:38.790
show that even 10
minutes or even better

01:38:38.790 --> 01:38:41.490
would be 20 or 30 minutes
of lying motionless

01:38:41.490 --> 01:38:45.300
with eyes closed and
allowing the mind to drift,

01:38:45.300 --> 01:38:49.140
wherever it happens
to go, but focusing

01:38:49.140 --> 01:38:51.690
on relaxing by doing
long exhale breathing,

01:38:51.690 --> 01:38:53.610
perhaps doing a body
scan or focusing

01:38:53.610 --> 01:38:55.830
your attention on
particular body parts

01:38:55.830 --> 01:38:58.230
but not keeping it focused
on any one particular body

01:38:58.230 --> 01:39:01.860
part for that long, that general
practice of deep relaxation

01:39:01.860 --> 01:39:04.920
while awake and being
relatively motionless really

01:39:04.920 --> 01:39:06.950
favors the brain
states associated

01:39:06.950 --> 01:39:07.950
with divergent thinking.

01:39:07.950 --> 01:39:10.620
And actually
represents an accessing

01:39:10.620 --> 01:39:12.660
of the various
components that you would

01:39:12.660 --> 01:39:14.880
use during divergent thinking.

01:39:14.880 --> 01:39:17.400
And perhaps most
excitingly, it's

01:39:17.400 --> 01:39:20.790
associated with this massive
increase, 65% increase

01:39:20.790 --> 01:39:23.400
in dopamine release within
the very pathway that

01:39:23.400 --> 01:39:25.233
underlies divergent thinking.

01:39:25.233 --> 01:39:27.150
So my recommendation
would be for those of you

01:39:27.150 --> 01:39:29.025
that are trying to
enhance divergent thinking

01:39:29.025 --> 01:39:32.410
and creative ability, that
you would do this practice

01:39:32.410 --> 01:39:34.013
at a minimum once per week.

01:39:34.013 --> 01:39:36.430
And I should say if you were
going to do it once per week,

01:39:36.430 --> 01:39:38.920
I would recommend doing it
for about 20 to 30 minutes.

01:39:38.920 --> 01:39:41.890
Some of you might be able to do
it for as long as 60 minutes.

01:39:41.890 --> 01:39:44.890
I myself do such a practice
on a daily basis, anywhere

01:39:44.890 --> 01:39:48.460
from 10 minutes to 20
minutes, sometimes 30 minutes.

01:39:48.460 --> 01:39:52.600
There's an example of an NSDR
script, completely zero cost.

01:39:52.600 --> 01:39:54.610
I confess it does
happen to be my voice.

01:39:54.610 --> 01:39:56.470
So forgive me in advance.

01:39:56.470 --> 01:39:58.000
There are other options of NSDR.

01:39:58.000 --> 01:40:00.820
You can go to YouTube,
put NSDR and my name.

01:40:00.820 --> 01:40:02.140
Again, completely zero cost.

01:40:02.140 --> 01:40:05.320
You can get a sample of
what a 10 minute NSDR

01:40:05.320 --> 01:40:06.220
script looks like.

01:40:06.220 --> 01:40:09.140
That's through Virtusan,
put that out there.

01:40:09.140 --> 01:40:11.140
So thank you Virtusan for
putting that out there

01:40:11.140 --> 01:40:12.250
at zero cost.

01:40:12.250 --> 01:40:16.600
There are examples of 20 and
30 minute NSDR scripts and yoga

01:40:16.600 --> 01:40:17.350
nidra scripts.

01:40:17.350 --> 01:40:18.800
Some that I particularly like.

01:40:18.800 --> 01:40:20.800
We will also provide a
link to some of those.

01:40:20.800 --> 01:40:23.620
Again, those are completely
zero cost for you to explore.

01:40:23.620 --> 01:40:26.800
But more important than you
follow any one particular yoga

01:40:26.800 --> 01:40:28.960
nidra NSDR script
is that you learn

01:40:28.960 --> 01:40:34.000
to take your body and brain into
these states of limited motion,

01:40:34.000 --> 01:40:36.610
elevated dopamine within
this particular pathway,

01:40:36.610 --> 01:40:38.230
and fairly deep relaxation.

01:40:38.230 --> 01:40:39.910
Again, if you happen
to fall asleep,

01:40:39.910 --> 01:40:41.913
that's not necessarily
a bad thing,

01:40:41.913 --> 01:40:43.330
although the idea
is that you stay

01:40:43.330 --> 01:40:45.130
in a shallow plane
of consciousness

01:40:45.130 --> 01:40:49.120
or sleep hence the term
nonsleep deep rest.

01:40:49.120 --> 01:40:51.670
So in any event, I think this
is a very useful practice

01:40:51.670 --> 01:40:53.170
that many people
could benefit from.

01:40:53.170 --> 01:40:55.462
And the fact that it's zero
cost and purely behavioral,

01:40:55.462 --> 01:40:58.355
I think adds additional
benefit because it's

01:40:58.355 --> 01:40:59.980
certainly one that
people could explore

01:40:59.980 --> 01:41:02.800
depending on what amount of
time you're willing to commit.

01:41:02.800 --> 01:41:05.290
And the research
data on this now

01:41:05.290 --> 01:41:07.420
extend beyond this
one individual paper.

01:41:07.420 --> 01:41:10.060
And I think is really
exciting because what it says

01:41:10.060 --> 01:41:12.610
is as the title and
first line of the paper

01:41:12.610 --> 01:41:14.740
suggests is that we
can increase dopamine

01:41:14.740 --> 01:41:17.470
using specific types
of meditation induced

01:41:17.470 --> 01:41:18.250
consciousness.

01:41:18.250 --> 01:41:20.470
And those increases
in dopamine can

01:41:20.470 --> 01:41:23.320
be used to increase our
ability to be more creative.

01:41:23.320 --> 01:41:26.230
Before moving forward, I
want to make absolutely clear

01:41:26.230 --> 01:41:29.320
how it is that you
would use an NSDR a.k.a.

01:41:29.320 --> 01:41:31.900
yoga nidra or similar,
the name doesn't really

01:41:31.900 --> 01:41:34.660
matter after all, the
practice is what matters,

01:41:34.660 --> 01:41:39.160
in order to enhance dopamine
in this nigrostriatal pathway

01:41:39.160 --> 01:41:40.960
and enhance divergent thinking.

01:41:40.960 --> 01:41:43.120
The key thing to
understand here is

01:41:43.120 --> 01:41:46.120
that the period
of motionlessness

01:41:46.120 --> 01:41:49.600
and deep relaxation
while awake increases

01:41:49.600 --> 01:41:51.970
dopamine in the
nigrostriatal pathway.

01:41:51.970 --> 01:41:54.580
It increases mental imagery.

01:41:54.580 --> 01:41:59.290
That is, it increases access to
the bank or the library, if you

01:41:59.290 --> 01:42:02.770
will, of possible
solutions or elements

01:42:02.770 --> 01:42:05.350
to engage in the divergent
thinking process.

01:42:05.350 --> 01:42:07.870
But divergent thinking
itself does not

01:42:07.870 --> 01:42:10.660
occur during NSDR a.k.a.

01:42:10.660 --> 01:42:12.490
yoga nidra.

01:42:12.490 --> 01:42:16.130
The NSDR and yoga nidra, a
deep relaxation meditation,

01:42:16.130 --> 01:42:19.635
whatever it is you want to call
it, sets a dopaminergic tone.

01:42:19.635 --> 01:42:21.760
And that's actually the
appropriate use of the word

01:42:21.760 --> 01:42:24.100
dopaminergic tone it
raises the baseline

01:42:24.100 --> 01:42:27.370
of dopamine transmission
in that circuitry that

01:42:27.370 --> 01:42:30.670
then positions you to
engage in divergent thinking

01:42:30.670 --> 01:42:31.820
more effectively.

01:42:31.820 --> 01:42:34.900
So the idea would be to
do anywhere from 10 to 20,

01:42:34.900 --> 01:42:37.370
maybe 30 minutes, maybe
even as much as an hour,

01:42:37.370 --> 01:42:39.910
depending on how much time
you had to dedicate of such

01:42:39.910 --> 01:42:42.130
a meditation and NSDR practice.

01:42:42.130 --> 01:42:44.770
And then not
necessarily immediately

01:42:44.770 --> 01:42:48.790
but within the 5 to 15
minutes following, then

01:42:48.790 --> 01:42:51.580
to go into a practice
of divergent thinking

01:42:51.580 --> 01:42:53.500
and start doing
creative exploration.

01:42:53.500 --> 01:42:55.540
That is to start thinking
about different ways

01:42:55.540 --> 01:42:58.000
to combine existing
elements in whatever domain

01:42:58.000 --> 01:43:00.340
it is that you want
to achieve creativity.

01:43:00.340 --> 01:43:03.040
So the point is that the
divergent thinking itself

01:43:03.040 --> 01:43:07.480
is not occurring during the
NSDR or yoga nidra practice.

01:43:07.480 --> 01:43:09.940
The NSDR and yoga nidra
practice prepares you

01:43:09.940 --> 01:43:13.270
for divergent thinking that
you do in the hour or hours

01:43:13.270 --> 01:43:14.080
that follows.

01:43:14.080 --> 01:43:16.570
And just to contrast
that with pharmacology,

01:43:16.570 --> 01:43:20.860
I am not aware of any specific
dopamine related pharmacology

01:43:20.860 --> 01:43:23.530
that would allow us to
selectively increase dopamine

01:43:23.530 --> 01:43:25.990
in the very pathway associated
with divergent thinking

01:43:25.990 --> 01:43:27.640
and creativity.

01:43:27.640 --> 01:43:29.710
Now, there are forms
of pharmacology

01:43:29.710 --> 01:43:31.990
that can shift brain
neurotransmitters

01:43:31.990 --> 01:43:34.438
and modulators in ways
that favor creativity.

01:43:34.438 --> 01:43:35.980
And this is certainly
a topic that we

01:43:35.980 --> 01:43:38.620
we'll go into in more
depth in a future episode.

01:43:38.620 --> 01:43:40.120
But there's an
exciting study that

01:43:40.120 --> 01:43:42.400
was performed just
this last year looking

01:43:42.400 --> 01:43:45.910
at the role of serotonin,
another neuromodulator,

01:43:45.910 --> 01:43:48.160
in divergent and
convergent thinking.

01:43:48.160 --> 01:43:53.020
And it turns out that serotonin
underlies a lot of the brain

01:43:53.020 --> 01:43:55.660
activity that's responsible
for both divergent

01:43:55.660 --> 01:43:57.370
and for convergent thinking.

01:43:57.370 --> 01:44:00.520
And there's one particular
form of pharmacology

01:44:00.520 --> 01:44:04.060
which can enhance activation
of the serotonergic pathways

01:44:04.060 --> 01:44:07.720
associated with the so-called
5-HT, that's serotonin.

01:44:07.720 --> 01:44:09.880
5-HT, that's the abbreviation.

01:44:09.880 --> 01:44:12.880
5-HT2A receptor.

01:44:12.880 --> 01:44:15.910
Serotonin 2A receptor in
particular brain areas

01:44:15.910 --> 01:44:18.460
in ways that favor both
divergent and convergent

01:44:18.460 --> 01:44:19.030
thinking.

01:44:19.030 --> 01:44:21.940
And the pharmacologic
agent in that case

01:44:21.940 --> 01:44:24.970
turns out to be very low
dose or as some of you

01:44:24.970 --> 01:44:29.383
may have heard of it referred
to as microdosing of psilocybin.

01:44:29.383 --> 01:44:32.050
Now, I do want to say because it
would be entirely inappropriate

01:44:32.050 --> 01:44:35.747
for me to not say this, that
in most areas of the world

01:44:35.747 --> 01:44:37.330
and particularly in
the United States,

01:44:37.330 --> 01:44:39.250
psilocybin is still illegal.

01:44:39.250 --> 01:44:40.420
It is not legal.

01:44:40.420 --> 01:44:42.970
In some areas it has
been decriminalized

01:44:42.970 --> 01:44:45.640
and there are a number of
different clinical trials

01:44:45.640 --> 01:44:48.250
occurring now at Johns
Hopkins, at Stanford,

01:44:48.250 --> 01:44:50.260
at University of
California, San Francisco,

01:44:50.260 --> 01:44:52.150
and elsewhere
exploring psilocybin

01:44:52.150 --> 01:44:54.880
for the treatment of
depression, for trauma,

01:44:54.880 --> 01:44:56.530
for eating disorders.

01:44:56.530 --> 01:45:00.250
Most of those studies focus
on macrodoses of psilocybin

01:45:00.250 --> 01:45:01.300
not microdosing.

01:45:01.300 --> 01:45:03.850
There are far fewer studies
of microdosing of psilocybin.

01:45:03.850 --> 01:45:07.090
And I do have to point
out that psilocybin use

01:45:07.090 --> 01:45:10.340
and possession and, of
course, sale is still illegal.

01:45:10.340 --> 01:45:12.860
So I would be remiss
if I didn't state that.

01:45:12.860 --> 01:45:15.340
However, I will provide
a link to the study that

01:45:15.340 --> 01:45:17.620
shows that microdosing
of psilocybin

01:45:17.620 --> 01:45:20.540
for a series of weeks
on a daily basis.

01:45:20.540 --> 01:45:22.060
So these are dosages
of psilocybin

01:45:22.060 --> 01:45:25.930
that do not induce hallucination
and do not massively

01:45:25.930 --> 01:45:30.790
shift mood or internal states in
any way that has people feeling

01:45:30.790 --> 01:45:33.145
like they are acting
or feeling that

01:45:33.145 --> 01:45:35.020
much different although
some people do report

01:45:35.020 --> 01:45:36.340
a subjective shift.

01:45:36.340 --> 01:45:40.060
Does seem to increase
divergent thinking ability.

01:45:40.060 --> 01:45:43.720
But I do want to put a
big asterisks, a highlight

01:45:43.720 --> 01:45:47.120
in an underlying beneath the
statement I'm about to make,

01:45:47.120 --> 01:45:50.290
which is that pharmacology
of the serotonin system

01:45:50.290 --> 01:45:53.440
just as pharmacology
of the dopamine system

01:45:53.440 --> 01:45:55.060
is very broadband.

01:45:55.060 --> 01:45:56.080
It's a shotgun approach.

01:45:56.080 --> 01:45:57.580
You're going to hit
all the circuits

01:45:57.580 --> 01:45:59.080
of the brain that
involve serotonin

01:45:59.080 --> 01:46:00.910
with microdosing psilocybin.

01:46:00.910 --> 01:46:04.240
Although it has some selectivity
for the 5-HT2A receptor

01:46:04.240 --> 01:46:08.390
it can attach to other
receptors as well and act there.

01:46:08.390 --> 01:46:10.810
This is the same reason why
SSRI, Selective Serotonin

01:46:10.810 --> 01:46:14.260
Reuptake Inhibitors can
indeed shift mood and appetite

01:46:14.260 --> 01:46:16.720
but it can also shift
libido and other things.

01:46:16.720 --> 01:46:19.390
It's because there are
serotonin receptors everywhere,

01:46:19.390 --> 01:46:21.982
or I should say many
places, not just in

01:46:21.982 --> 01:46:24.190
the areas of the brain that
are associated with mood,

01:46:24.190 --> 01:46:25.610
for instance.

01:46:25.610 --> 01:46:29.740
And as I mentioned before,
agents, whether or not they

01:46:29.740 --> 01:46:33.100
are recreational or illicit
drugs or prescription drugs

01:46:33.100 --> 01:46:36.610
or supplements that
increase dopamine will also

01:46:36.610 --> 01:46:39.950
be broadband into a number of
different circuits in parallel.

01:46:39.950 --> 01:46:44.020
So this is why I always
say behavioral tools really

01:46:44.020 --> 01:46:45.220
should come first.

01:46:45.220 --> 01:46:47.555
I don't say that because
I dislike pharmacology,

01:46:47.555 --> 01:46:49.930
I say that because in many
cases behavioral tools are not

01:46:49.930 --> 01:46:52.450
only safer and easier
to titrate to adjust

01:46:52.450 --> 01:46:55.330
the duration, et cetera
than is pharmacology,

01:46:55.330 --> 01:46:57.412
but also because
they can sometimes

01:46:57.412 --> 01:46:58.870
as in the case of
the study we just

01:46:58.870 --> 01:47:02.770
described for you more
specificity not less than

01:47:02.770 --> 01:47:03.530
pharmacology.

01:47:03.530 --> 01:47:06.520
Pharmacology has its
place can be wonderful,

01:47:06.520 --> 01:47:08.440
provided safe and
legal, et cetera.

01:47:08.440 --> 01:47:11.842
But it can cause a lot of
so-called off target effects.

01:47:11.842 --> 01:47:14.050
So for those of you that
are interested in increasing

01:47:14.050 --> 01:47:16.720
creativity through
pharmacology, I

01:47:16.720 --> 01:47:20.620
would say, stay tuned for
the data on psilocybin

01:47:20.620 --> 01:47:21.880
and microdosing psilocybin.

01:47:21.880 --> 01:47:25.930
If you are absolutely obsessed
with the idea of microdosing

01:47:25.930 --> 01:47:27.708
psilocybin for
enhancing creativity

01:47:27.708 --> 01:47:29.500
and you'd like to go
straight to the study,

01:47:29.500 --> 01:47:32.020
I will tell you what that
study is and therefore you

01:47:32.020 --> 01:47:34.450
can access some of the
specifics in terms of dosages

01:47:34.450 --> 01:47:35.560
and protocols, et cetera.

01:47:35.560 --> 01:47:36.977
So since I can't
help myself, I'll

01:47:36.977 --> 01:47:41.180
just very briefly summarize that
microdosing psychedelic study.

01:47:41.180 --> 01:47:44.380
The title of the study,
which was published in 2018

01:47:44.380 --> 01:47:46.600
is Exploring the Effect of
Microdosing Psychedelics

01:47:46.600 --> 01:47:50.260
on Creativity in an Open
Label Natural Setting.

01:47:50.260 --> 01:47:51.220
Interesting title.

01:47:51.220 --> 01:47:53.530
This was a microdosing
event organized

01:47:53.530 --> 01:47:55.600
by the Dutch
psychedelic society.

01:47:55.600 --> 01:47:58.240
They examined the effects
of psychedelic truffles

01:47:58.240 --> 01:48:02.650
where they knew what psychedelic
compounds were contained there

01:48:02.650 --> 01:48:06.130
on two creativity related
problem solving task,

01:48:06.130 --> 01:48:09.460
the picture concept task, which
I don't expect you to recognize

01:48:09.460 --> 01:48:11.830
or know but it assesses
convergent thinking

01:48:11.830 --> 01:48:14.447
and the alternative
uses task which

01:48:14.447 --> 01:48:16.030
I also don't expect
you to know but is

01:48:16.030 --> 01:48:18.670
a standard task for
assessing divergent thinking.

01:48:18.670 --> 01:48:21.070
They tested once before
taking a microdose

01:48:21.070 --> 01:48:23.920
and while the effects were
expected to be manifested

01:48:23.920 --> 01:48:24.790
they say.

01:48:24.790 --> 01:48:25.300
Interesting.

01:48:25.300 --> 01:48:27.633
They use the word manifested
in a study of psychedelics.

01:48:27.633 --> 01:48:30.250
Science is changing indeed.

01:48:30.250 --> 01:48:36.130
In any case, what they found
was an enhancement of creative.

01:48:36.130 --> 01:48:39.250
That is, divergent and
convergent thinking

01:48:39.250 --> 01:48:44.050
not surprising given the
fact that the 5-HT2A receptor

01:48:44.050 --> 01:48:48.460
activity is increased by
microdosing of psilocybin

01:48:48.460 --> 01:48:52.450
and 5-HT2A receptors are present
both on the neural circuits

01:48:52.450 --> 01:48:55.540
that underlie divergent
and convergent thinking.

01:48:55.540 --> 01:49:00.340
So, again, this is not a plug
for microdosing psilocybin.

01:49:00.340 --> 01:49:02.315
This is really in
response to what

01:49:02.315 --> 01:49:04.690
I know will be a number of
different questions about what

01:49:04.690 --> 01:49:08.020
pharmacologic agents can be
used to increase creativity.

01:49:08.020 --> 01:49:09.910
So more on that later
and, again, we'll

01:49:09.910 --> 01:49:13.840
provide a link if you want to
read that study in more depth.

01:49:13.840 --> 01:49:15.310
I can imagine that
a number of you

01:49:15.310 --> 01:49:17.940
are probably also wondering
about the effects of alcohol

01:49:17.940 --> 01:49:19.690
and the effects of
cannabis on creativity.

01:49:19.690 --> 01:49:22.870
We did a long in-depth
episode all about alcohol

01:49:22.870 --> 01:49:24.850
and its effects on health.

01:49:24.850 --> 01:49:28.055
The bottom line on
alcohol is that in excess

01:49:28.055 --> 01:49:29.680
of two drinks per
week, you're starting

01:49:29.680 --> 01:49:33.550
to run into the cancer promoting
and toxic effects of alcohol.

01:49:33.550 --> 01:49:36.620
I didn't choose for
the answer to be that,

01:49:36.620 --> 01:49:39.460
but that's what
the data tell us.

01:49:39.460 --> 01:49:41.830
I'm not telling you you can't
drink more than two drinks

01:49:41.830 --> 01:49:43.580
per week, I'm just saying that
if you're going to do that,

01:49:43.580 --> 01:49:45.640
you should really
consider offsetting

01:49:45.640 --> 01:49:47.620
that with some other
behavioral measures

01:49:47.620 --> 01:49:49.390
all discussed in the
episode on alcohol.

01:49:49.390 --> 01:49:52.120
And despite what
people think, there

01:49:52.120 --> 01:49:57.310
is absolutely zero evidence that
alcohol increases creativity.

01:49:57.310 --> 01:50:00.550
However, by way of
reducing activation

01:50:00.550 --> 01:50:03.280
of the prefrontal cortex,
there is some evidence

01:50:03.280 --> 01:50:05.260
that alcohol and
other substances

01:50:05.260 --> 01:50:08.650
that reduce what's called
autobiographical scripting,

01:50:08.650 --> 01:50:12.500
that is, a narrative
about ourselves,

01:50:12.500 --> 01:50:15.970
so self awareness, that it
can enhance divergent thinking

01:50:15.970 --> 01:50:17.435
at very low doses.

01:50:17.435 --> 01:50:18.310
And this makes sense.

01:50:18.310 --> 01:50:21.430
Divergent thinking involves
remembering certain things

01:50:21.430 --> 01:50:23.770
that we can use as elements
in the creative process

01:50:23.770 --> 01:50:26.890
but suppressing narratives
about what the use of those

01:50:26.890 --> 01:50:27.460
would mean.

01:50:27.460 --> 01:50:28.360
Will people like it?

01:50:28.360 --> 01:50:29.410
Will they not like it?

01:50:29.410 --> 01:50:30.550
Will it to the outcome we want?

01:50:30.550 --> 01:50:31.180
Well, it won't.

01:50:31.180 --> 01:50:33.010
All of that
autobiographical scripting

01:50:33.010 --> 01:50:36.160
involves the forebrain
being very, very active

01:50:36.160 --> 01:50:38.860
and specific regions of the
forebrain in particular.

01:50:38.860 --> 01:50:41.560
And that all needs
to be suppressed,

01:50:41.560 --> 01:50:44.370
which alcohol in very
low doses can accomplish.

01:50:44.370 --> 01:50:46.120
But, again, that's not
a plug for alcohol.

01:50:46.120 --> 01:50:48.490
I think behavioral tools
would be a much better route.

01:50:48.490 --> 01:50:51.160
But therefore it
shouldn't be surprising

01:50:51.160 --> 01:50:53.740
why some people have used
low dose alcohol in order

01:50:53.740 --> 01:50:55.540
to engage in the
creative process

01:50:55.540 --> 01:50:57.190
because it involves
less inhibition

01:50:57.190 --> 01:50:59.890
or sense of self that could be
detrimental to the divergent

01:50:59.890 --> 01:51:00.910
thinking process.

01:51:00.910 --> 01:51:04.120
Now, with respect to
cannabis, I went in depth

01:51:04.120 --> 01:51:07.540
into the biology and the
various uses, misuses,

01:51:07.540 --> 01:51:10.720
dangers, and in some cases
benefits of cannabis use

01:51:10.720 --> 01:51:12.100
in certain.

01:51:12.100 --> 01:51:14.630
The key word there is
certain populations.

01:51:14.630 --> 01:51:17.620
And I also dove
into whether or not

01:51:17.620 --> 01:51:20.300
cannabis can be used to increase
divergent and convergent

01:51:20.300 --> 01:51:20.800
thinking.

01:51:20.800 --> 01:51:22.270
So that's timestamped
in that episode.

01:51:22.270 --> 01:51:23.562
I'll refer you to that episode.

01:51:23.562 --> 01:51:26.890
But the long and short of
it is that many of the ideas

01:51:26.890 --> 01:51:30.160
that people come up with when
under the influence of cannabis

01:51:30.160 --> 01:51:35.530
in particular high THC
containing cannabis does lead

01:51:35.530 --> 01:51:39.220
to enhanced divergent
thinking, but so enhanced

01:51:39.220 --> 01:51:41.800
it turns out that
oftentimes those ideas can't

01:51:41.800 --> 01:51:44.270
be constrained by the
convergent thinking process.

01:51:44.270 --> 01:51:45.910
In other words, they
have lots of ideas

01:51:45.910 --> 01:51:49.570
that make sense while under
the influence of cannabis.

01:51:49.570 --> 01:51:53.830
But that later cannot be
implemented into a coherent

01:51:53.830 --> 01:51:58.300
framework that leads to any
actual creative endeavor

01:51:58.300 --> 01:51:59.920
or creative product.

01:51:59.920 --> 01:52:02.170
Or, as is often the
case with cannabis,

01:52:02.170 --> 01:52:04.780
they simply can't remember
what they were thinking about.

01:52:04.780 --> 01:52:07.030
Any time there's a
discussion about dopamine,

01:52:07.030 --> 01:52:09.910
there seems to be a discussion
about motivation, desire,

01:52:09.910 --> 01:52:10.540
and drive.

01:52:10.540 --> 01:52:13.090
And, of course, that makes sense
given the roles of dopamine.

01:52:13.090 --> 01:52:15.180
We did an entire episode
on dopamine, motivation,

01:52:15.180 --> 01:52:15.680
and drive.

01:52:15.680 --> 01:52:17.470
It's one of our most
popular episodes.

01:52:17.470 --> 01:52:20.440
Again, you can access that
with timestamps and all formats

01:52:20.440 --> 01:52:22.150
at hubermanlab.com.

01:52:22.150 --> 01:52:23.590
And any time
there's a discussion

01:52:23.590 --> 01:52:26.950
about dopamine and
motivation, we also

01:52:26.950 --> 01:52:30.220
seem to have a lot of questions
about attention and focus

01:52:30.220 --> 01:52:32.980
and ADHD or attention
deficit hyperactivity

01:52:32.980 --> 01:52:34.580
disorder in particular.

01:52:34.580 --> 01:52:36.520
So just as a brief
mention, there

01:52:36.520 --> 01:52:39.400
is a literature although
not terribly extensive,

01:52:39.400 --> 01:52:42.580
a small but strong literature
on the relationship

01:52:42.580 --> 01:52:44.890
between ADHD and creativity.

01:52:44.890 --> 01:52:46.630
And the long and short
of that literature

01:52:46.630 --> 01:52:50.500
is that people who have
ADHD regardless of age

01:52:50.500 --> 01:52:53.110
do seem to have an
ability to focus.

01:52:53.110 --> 01:52:55.090
I've mentioned that
in the episode on ADHD

01:52:55.090 --> 01:52:57.790
provided that they are
interested in the thing

01:52:57.790 --> 01:52:59.090
that they are focusing on.

01:52:59.090 --> 01:53:01.570
So that runs counter to this
idea that people with ADHD

01:53:01.570 --> 01:53:02.530
simply can't focus.

01:53:02.530 --> 01:53:05.620
They can but it tends to be a
focus that selective for things

01:53:05.620 --> 01:53:08.470
that they are very excited about
or interested in as opposed

01:53:08.470 --> 01:53:10.930
to a general ability to focus.

01:53:10.930 --> 01:53:13.750
What's also highly
underappreciated

01:53:13.750 --> 01:53:17.260
is that people who
have ADHD oftentimes

01:53:17.260 --> 01:53:19.900
are very effective
at divergent thinking

01:53:19.900 --> 01:53:22.720
but are less effective
at convergent thinking.

01:53:22.720 --> 01:53:25.000
What this tells us is
that people with ADHD

01:53:25.000 --> 01:53:29.500
can often have excellent novel
and indeed creative ideas,

01:53:29.500 --> 01:53:32.320
but that the implementation
of those creative ideas

01:53:32.320 --> 01:53:33.430
is sometimes challenge.

01:53:33.430 --> 01:53:36.220
And that's one reason to
explore rational pharmacology,

01:53:36.220 --> 01:53:38.050
nutrition,
supplementation, et cetera.

01:53:38.050 --> 01:53:40.150
Those are all things
to explore in concert

01:53:40.150 --> 01:53:43.330
with or I should say in
working closely with a board

01:53:43.330 --> 01:53:46.948
certified physician or ideally
psychiatrist expert in ADHD.

01:53:46.948 --> 01:53:49.240
You can also check out the
episode that we did on ADHD.

01:53:49.240 --> 01:53:50.532
There are a lot of tools there.

01:53:50.532 --> 01:53:53.200
A lot of science mentioned
there to support those tools.

01:53:53.200 --> 01:53:55.150
Again, you can find
that Hubermanlab.com.

01:53:55.150 --> 01:53:57.350
But I did think it was
important to point out,

01:53:57.350 --> 01:54:01.990
even if briefly that having ADHD
is not a barrier to creativity

01:54:01.990 --> 01:54:05.710
and, in fact, may actually be
an enhanced portal to creativity

01:54:05.710 --> 01:54:08.440
but that it doesn't
allow people to access

01:54:08.440 --> 01:54:12.100
the convergent thinking that
allows creative ideas to be

01:54:12.100 --> 01:54:14.950
implemented into specific
strategies, pressure tested,

01:54:14.950 --> 01:54:17.920
and eventually delivered in
the form of a final product

01:54:17.920 --> 01:54:19.630
of music, art, et cetera.

01:54:19.630 --> 01:54:22.690
That is not to say that people
with ADHD cannot accomplish

01:54:22.690 --> 01:54:25.630
that, but that it is going to
require some additional steps

01:54:25.630 --> 01:54:28.810
and protocols in order to
enhance convergent thinking.

01:54:28.810 --> 01:54:30.490
And that episode and
the episode that we

01:54:30.490 --> 01:54:34.300
did on focus and in particular
tools to enhance focus

01:54:34.300 --> 01:54:38.260
is very much directed at ways
to enhance convergent thinking.

01:54:38.260 --> 01:54:40.450
So if you have ADHD or
know somebody who does

01:54:40.450 --> 01:54:42.367
and you're interested
in the creative process,

01:54:42.367 --> 01:54:44.770
we're focusing generally
please check out the episodes

01:54:44.770 --> 01:54:45.580
that I mentioned.

01:54:45.580 --> 01:54:47.980
Now, there's also a small
but nonetheless very exciting

01:54:47.980 --> 01:54:50.740
literature on the relationship
between physical movement

01:54:50.740 --> 01:54:52.130
and divergent thinking.

01:54:52.130 --> 01:54:54.280
This should come as
no surprise to us.

01:54:54.280 --> 01:54:56.530
As mentioned many times
now in this episode,

01:54:56.530 --> 01:54:59.170
the nigrostriatal pathway
involved in divergent thinking

01:54:59.170 --> 01:55:02.560
that involves dopamine is also
responsible for eye blinks

01:55:02.560 --> 01:55:05.920
and for movements of the
limbs of the body in very

01:55:05.920 --> 01:55:07.360
deliberate ways.

01:55:07.360 --> 01:55:10.750
This tells us that there's
some direct or maybe

01:55:10.750 --> 01:55:12.670
indirect relationship
between movement

01:55:12.670 --> 01:55:15.207
of the body and
divergent thinking.

01:55:15.207 --> 01:55:17.290
And despite the fact that
it's only a few studies,

01:55:17.290 --> 01:55:19.900
there have been some studies
of whether or not people are

01:55:19.900 --> 01:55:21.700
able to engage in
divergent thinking

01:55:21.700 --> 01:55:23.860
more effectively when
they are doing things

01:55:23.860 --> 01:55:26.277
like pacing or walking.

01:55:26.277 --> 01:55:28.360
And this could be on a
treadmill or back and forth

01:55:28.360 --> 01:55:29.170
across the room.

01:55:29.170 --> 01:55:31.360
And in fact, that is
absolutely the case.

01:55:31.360 --> 01:55:33.250
If you're somebody
like myself who

01:55:33.250 --> 01:55:36.550
tends to have their
best ideas, not

01:55:36.550 --> 01:55:39.580
saying that my ideas are always
terrific, but among the ideas

01:55:39.580 --> 01:55:42.280
I have, some of the better
ones arrive to me while

01:55:42.280 --> 01:55:44.080
on my long Sunday run.

01:55:44.080 --> 01:55:46.630
I tend to do a long
run or hike on Sundays

01:55:46.630 --> 01:55:49.580
sometimes with a light weight
vest or something of that sort.

01:55:49.580 --> 01:55:52.300
But when I'm in a state
of essentially not

01:55:52.300 --> 01:55:54.730
directing my attention
to any one thing

01:55:54.730 --> 01:55:57.475
in my external environment, this
is extremely key for reasons

01:55:57.475 --> 01:55:58.600
that now should be obvious.

01:55:58.600 --> 01:56:00.225
Any time we are
directing our attention

01:56:00.225 --> 01:56:02.770
to a visual target
or an auditory target

01:56:02.770 --> 01:56:05.380
we are not as able to engage
in divergent thinking.

01:56:05.380 --> 01:56:07.420
This is why I will
sometimes listen

01:56:07.420 --> 01:56:10.090
to podcasts or to audiobooks
while I go on these runs.

01:56:10.090 --> 01:56:11.740
But for portions of
these runs or hikes

01:56:11.740 --> 01:56:15.070
I tend to turn those off and
just focus on the movement

01:56:15.070 --> 01:56:17.650
and focus on not focusing
on anything in particular.

01:56:17.650 --> 01:56:21.190
And oftentimes I will stop and
write down ideas that suddenly

01:56:21.190 --> 01:56:25.120
or seemingly suddenly appear
to me or geyser to the surface.

01:56:25.120 --> 01:56:25.870
I'll have an idea.

01:56:25.870 --> 01:56:28.300
Sometimes those are good ideas,
sometimes less good ideas.

01:56:28.300 --> 01:56:30.370
The fact that happens
for me and the fact

01:56:30.370 --> 01:56:33.040
that many people are
pacers or runners

01:56:33.040 --> 01:56:35.350
or come up with their best
ideas while in the shower

01:56:35.350 --> 01:56:38.020
or while engaging in
activities that don't require

01:56:38.020 --> 01:56:39.880
a lot of sensory
attention to one

01:56:39.880 --> 01:56:42.580
specific location either
visual or auditory,

01:56:42.580 --> 01:56:47.290
et cetera, that is because it
engages these nigrostriatal

01:56:47.290 --> 01:56:50.530
pathways through movement
which then opens up

01:56:50.530 --> 01:56:53.380
this library of ideas and
allows the intersection

01:56:53.380 --> 01:56:55.900
of different ideas that
normally would be constrained

01:56:55.900 --> 01:56:57.460
to separate categories.

01:56:57.460 --> 01:56:59.530
One way to think
about this by analogy

01:56:59.530 --> 01:57:02.620
would be when I was a kid
you'd go to the library,

01:57:02.620 --> 01:57:03.790
nowadays you just go online.

01:57:03.790 --> 01:57:05.740
But the different pages
of different books

01:57:05.740 --> 01:57:08.890
on different topics are kept
distinct from one another.

01:57:08.890 --> 01:57:11.680
That is bound by different
book covers and book

01:57:11.680 --> 01:57:14.807
ends, different
shelves in the library.

01:57:14.807 --> 01:57:17.140
It's as if different pages
and elements from those books

01:57:17.140 --> 01:57:19.960
are now being combined
in a pseudo random.

01:57:19.960 --> 01:57:22.480
Not random but in a
pseudo random way.

01:57:22.480 --> 01:57:25.480
And in that combination,
new possibilities

01:57:25.480 --> 01:57:27.580
about ways that information
could be combined

01:57:27.580 --> 01:57:30.050
and implemented start to arise.

01:57:30.050 --> 01:57:32.320
So the tool that emerges
from this is very simple.

01:57:32.320 --> 01:57:34.790
And it won't necessarily
apply to everybody.

01:57:34.790 --> 01:57:36.310
But if you are
somebody who finds

01:57:36.310 --> 01:57:38.560
that just sitting in a chair
and trying to be creative

01:57:38.560 --> 01:57:41.025
is very challenging, some
of you might benefit from,

01:57:41.025 --> 01:57:42.400
for instance, if
you are engaging

01:57:42.400 --> 01:57:46.420
in writing or you want to
write to talk into the voice

01:57:46.420 --> 01:57:49.060
recorder of your phone while
walking or simply walking

01:57:49.060 --> 01:57:51.910
and not attending to any
one specific thing visually

01:57:51.910 --> 01:57:52.870
or through headphones.

01:57:52.870 --> 01:57:56.710
And then as ideas
surface, seemingly

01:57:56.710 --> 01:57:58.483
out of nowhere, which
is how it happens,

01:57:58.483 --> 01:58:00.400
that you could either
put them into your phone

01:58:00.400 --> 01:58:02.817
by voice dictation or you could
type them out if you like.

01:58:02.817 --> 01:58:04.420
The key thing is to
not be distracted

01:58:04.420 --> 01:58:05.842
by other things
in your phone, not

01:58:05.842 --> 01:58:08.050
to start going onto social
media or doing phone calls

01:58:08.050 --> 01:58:10.720
or looking at text messages
because that by definition

01:58:10.720 --> 01:58:13.990
is going to take you out
of this what biologists

01:58:13.990 --> 01:58:15.910
call a pseudo random walk.

01:58:15.910 --> 01:58:19.940
And this pseudo random element
is extremely important.

01:58:19.940 --> 01:58:22.690
We know, for instance, that
many circuits within the brain

01:58:22.690 --> 01:58:25.210
have what's called dedicated
point to point wiring.

01:58:25.210 --> 01:58:26.815
So, for instance,
the brain circuits

01:58:26.815 --> 01:58:27.940
that govern your breathing.

01:58:27.940 --> 01:58:29.857
The brain circuits that
govern your heartbeat.

01:58:29.857 --> 01:58:32.420
The brain circuits that
govern your specific movements

01:58:32.420 --> 01:58:35.590
once you are an adult and allow
for smooth directed movement

01:58:35.590 --> 01:58:39.550
are very precise, very little
slop if any in the wiring.

01:58:39.550 --> 01:58:43.750
However, there are aspects of
your brain circuitry, yours

01:58:43.750 --> 01:58:46.730
and everybody
else's I should say,

01:58:46.730 --> 01:58:49.180
that are maintained
into adulthood that

01:58:49.180 --> 01:58:52.090
include a lot of extra wiring.

01:58:52.090 --> 01:58:53.590
And these are fine wires.

01:58:53.590 --> 01:58:55.970
They are not the major highways
between different areas,

01:58:55.970 --> 01:58:56.540
if you will.

01:58:56.540 --> 01:59:01.090
So like Google Maps has highways
and streets and little passages

01:59:01.090 --> 01:59:01.750
and alleys.

01:59:01.750 --> 01:59:03.700
But it's as if
there's a little web

01:59:03.700 --> 01:59:07.540
of additional possible pathways
cast over that entire thing.

01:59:07.540 --> 01:59:11.710
The human brain maintains
such webs of possible passage.

01:59:11.710 --> 01:59:16.250
And it's only during activities
such as walking, running,

01:59:16.250 --> 01:59:20.760
cycling, swimming,
hiking, pacing, et

01:59:20.760 --> 01:59:24.760
cetera that the activation of
those pseudo random pathways

01:59:24.760 --> 01:59:26.240
starts to ramp up.

01:59:26.240 --> 01:59:28.990
So this is a purely
behavioral approach

01:59:28.990 --> 01:59:32.350
to engaging different elements
within neural networks that

01:59:32.350 --> 01:59:34.390
normally would not
communicate with one another

01:59:34.390 --> 01:59:35.830
when we are completely still.

01:59:35.830 --> 01:59:37.640
So again, the
practices that I talked

01:59:37.640 --> 01:59:39.640
about earlier of being
completely still to raise

01:59:39.640 --> 01:59:42.490
dopamine and enhanced
divergent thinking--

01:59:42.490 --> 01:59:45.760
I just want to
reemphasize are designed

01:59:45.760 --> 01:59:48.820
to position you to
ready you to engage

01:59:48.820 --> 01:59:51.220
in the activities like
walking and pacing,

01:59:51.220 --> 01:59:54.700
et cetera, that best
facilitate divergent thinking.

01:59:54.700 --> 01:59:57.610
So if you are somebody who wants
to enhance divergent thinking,

01:59:57.610 --> 01:59:59.500
I would encourage
you to explore how

01:59:59.500 --> 02:00:01.750
different patterns of movement
in particular, patterns

02:00:01.750 --> 02:00:04.180
of movement that don't require
any conscious attention

02:00:04.180 --> 02:00:08.650
to any one specific thing
allow you to access new ideas

02:00:08.650 --> 02:00:10.960
and new ways of combining
existing elements

02:00:10.960 --> 02:00:13.450
in whatever domain it is
you want to be creative.

02:00:13.450 --> 02:00:15.982
Now, this is also an opportunity
to underscore something

02:00:15.982 --> 02:00:17.440
I said back at the
beginning, which

02:00:17.440 --> 02:00:20.080
is you are not going to come
up with great works of music

02:00:20.080 --> 02:00:24.070
if you don't understand
chords and melodies and notes

02:00:24.070 --> 02:00:24.970
and music.

02:00:24.970 --> 02:00:26.890
Those basic elements
have to be built up

02:00:26.890 --> 02:00:30.152
through some formal or at least
rigorous or regular training.

02:00:30.152 --> 02:00:32.360
In the same way that you're
not going to take a walk,

02:00:32.360 --> 02:00:33.940
and then suddenly
be able to paint

02:00:33.940 --> 02:00:36.190
an incredible picture if you
have no painting ability.

02:00:36.190 --> 02:00:37.780
That is not going to happen.

02:00:37.780 --> 02:00:39.280
What I'm talking
about here are ways

02:00:39.280 --> 02:00:41.600
to enhance your capacity
for divergent thinking,

02:00:41.600 --> 02:00:45.680
such as NSDR and ways to
engage in divergent thinking,

02:00:45.680 --> 02:00:48.760
such as through certain forms
of movement that don't require

02:00:48.760 --> 02:00:51.340
a lot of conscious attention
to your surroundings or any one

02:00:51.340 --> 02:00:54.100
specific sensory target.

02:00:54.100 --> 02:00:56.980
And in doing so,
enhancing your ability

02:00:56.980 --> 02:00:59.860
to be more creative in a
domain for which you already

02:00:59.860 --> 02:01:02.650
have some degree of
skill or even mastery.

02:01:02.650 --> 02:01:04.240
Now, in keeping with
the theme of how

02:01:04.240 --> 02:01:06.520
to enhance our
creativity, there's

02:01:06.520 --> 02:01:11.110
a very exciting and yet parallel
literature to the literature

02:01:11.110 --> 02:01:12.688
that I've been
describing thus far.

02:01:12.688 --> 02:01:14.230
Now, I promise you
that I'm not going

02:01:14.230 --> 02:01:17.740
to open up an entire
library of new information

02:01:17.740 --> 02:01:19.700
related to neural
circuits and so forth.

02:01:19.700 --> 02:01:21.910
But I would be remiss
if I didn't mention

02:01:21.910 --> 02:01:25.030
this parallel literature because
it speaks very specifically

02:01:25.030 --> 02:01:28.630
to some important practices
that we can all use in order

02:01:28.630 --> 02:01:29.740
to enhance creativity.

02:01:29.740 --> 02:01:32.710
And to do so the first
time and every time.

02:01:32.710 --> 02:01:35.250
And this is really because
certain scientists out there

02:01:35.250 --> 02:01:37.140
have really gone
through the trouble.

02:01:37.140 --> 02:01:39.180
I should even say the
painstaking trouble

02:01:39.180 --> 02:01:42.000
of really trying to dissect
what the creative process is

02:01:42.000 --> 02:01:46.500
both for individuals and
in groups or even in pairs.

02:01:46.500 --> 02:01:49.590
And so what I'm about to tell
you is beautifully encapsulated

02:01:49.590 --> 02:01:51.810
in an article entitled--

02:01:51.810 --> 02:01:54.030
"A new method for
training creativity--

02:01:54.030 --> 02:01:57.120
narrative as an alternative
to divergent thinking."

02:01:57.120 --> 02:01:59.370
So again, we've been talking
about divergent thinking.

02:01:59.370 --> 02:02:01.830
That's one pathway into
the creative process

02:02:01.830 --> 02:02:03.400
but there are others as well.

02:02:03.400 --> 02:02:05.280
And as it turns
out, they're not so

02:02:05.280 --> 02:02:07.710
distinct in terms of the
underlying brain mechanisms.

02:02:07.710 --> 02:02:09.540
Nonetheless, let
me describe briefly

02:02:09.540 --> 02:02:12.360
how narrative can be
used to train creativity

02:02:12.360 --> 02:02:13.980
and to become more creative.

02:02:13.980 --> 02:02:15.540
And in order to
do that, I'd like

02:02:15.540 --> 02:02:17.220
to just briefly
paraphrase or read

02:02:17.220 --> 02:02:19.090
from the first
paragraph of this paper.

02:02:19.090 --> 02:02:22.170
So what I'm about to read are
the author's words, not mine.

02:02:22.170 --> 02:02:24.630
Quote "Here's a paradox.

02:02:24.630 --> 02:02:26.880
According to current
research young children

02:02:26.880 --> 02:02:29.460
are more imaginatively
creative than adults."

02:02:29.460 --> 02:02:31.590
And indeed that is
true, by the way.

02:02:31.590 --> 02:02:34.050
"Yet also according
to current research,

02:02:34.050 --> 02:02:37.560
creativity is main neural
engine is divergent thinking,

02:02:37.560 --> 02:02:40.800
which relies on memory
and logical association

02:02:40.800 --> 02:02:44.550
to tasks at which young
children underperform adults."

02:02:44.550 --> 02:02:48.090
That is, children are not as
good at divergent thinking

02:02:48.090 --> 02:02:48.840
as adults are.

02:02:48.840 --> 02:02:50.670
So how could it
be the authors are

02:02:50.670 --> 02:02:54.150
asking that children are
more imaginative and thus

02:02:54.150 --> 02:02:55.830
more creative than adults.

02:02:55.830 --> 02:02:59.340
This can only mean that
there are alternate pathways

02:02:59.340 --> 02:03:00.930
to creativity.

02:03:00.930 --> 02:03:02.680
And indeed that is the case.

02:03:02.680 --> 02:03:05.340
And so what this
paper really explores

02:03:05.340 --> 02:03:07.650
is other ways to
access creativity.

02:03:07.650 --> 02:03:10.710
And what they describe is
what's called narrative theory.

02:03:10.710 --> 02:03:12.480
And there's a number
of different aspects

02:03:12.480 --> 02:03:14.640
to this narrative theory.

02:03:14.640 --> 02:03:18.240
But they agree that the standard
definition of creativity

02:03:18.240 --> 02:03:20.530
is the same one that we
were talking about before.

02:03:20.530 --> 02:03:22.500
So we're not talking about a
different form of creativity,

02:03:22.500 --> 02:03:24.167
here we're talking
about a different way

02:03:24.167 --> 02:03:25.200
to access creativity.

02:03:25.200 --> 02:03:27.630
They describe the standard
definition of creativity

02:03:27.630 --> 02:03:29.280
as quote "The
ability to generate

02:03:29.280 --> 02:03:31.180
novel ideas that are useful."

02:03:31.180 --> 02:03:32.820
So the commonly accepted one.

02:03:32.820 --> 02:03:36.870
And what they cite as the
basis for narrative theory

02:03:36.870 --> 02:03:39.107
is this breakthrough
finding in the 1950s.

02:03:39.107 --> 02:03:41.190
This is the work of Guilford
Some people out there

02:03:41.190 --> 02:03:42.273
might be familiar with it.

02:03:42.273 --> 02:03:44.910
I was not at the outset of
researching this episode.

02:03:44.910 --> 02:03:47.497
What this theory from
Guilford essentially states

02:03:47.497 --> 02:03:49.830
is that there are different
intellectual capacities that

02:03:49.830 --> 02:03:51.548
are not captured by
standard IQ tests.

02:03:51.548 --> 02:03:53.340
I think that's generally
accepted nowadays.

02:03:53.340 --> 02:03:55.260
We know there's
emotional intelligence,

02:03:55.260 --> 02:03:57.630
we know the standard
IQ, et cetera.

02:03:57.630 --> 02:04:00.210
But the important
element to understand

02:04:00.210 --> 02:04:03.900
is that these authors
were able to trace back

02:04:03.900 --> 02:04:07.020
the idea of narrative
training as a way

02:04:07.020 --> 02:04:11.140
to enhance creativity long
before Guilford in the 1950s,

02:04:11.140 --> 02:04:13.273
all the way back to Aristotle.

02:04:13.273 --> 02:04:14.190
So this is incredible.

02:04:14.190 --> 02:04:15.900
Narrative theory
was actually birthed

02:04:15.900 --> 02:04:20.550
in 335 BCE in his
writing called Poetics,

02:04:20.550 --> 02:04:24.100
which I think is
incredible at least to me

02:04:24.100 --> 02:04:27.330
that people long before us
were thinking about creativity

02:04:27.330 --> 02:04:28.980
and what goes into creativity.

02:04:28.980 --> 02:04:32.310
And what Aristotle said, what
Guilford then elaborated on

02:04:32.310 --> 02:04:34.860
and what the authors
of this paper

02:04:34.860 --> 02:04:36.930
further elaborate
on and actually have

02:04:36.930 --> 02:04:38.670
developed training
protocols for,

02:04:38.670 --> 02:04:41.430
is the idea that there are
three elements that we can use

02:04:41.430 --> 02:04:42.900
in order to enhance creativity.

02:04:42.900 --> 02:04:45.360
And those three
elements are what's

02:04:45.360 --> 02:04:47.520
called world
building, I'll explain

02:04:47.520 --> 02:04:50.340
what these are in a moment,
perspective shifting,

02:04:50.340 --> 02:04:52.170
and action generating.

02:04:52.170 --> 02:04:54.960
And right off the
bat, the word action

02:04:54.960 --> 02:04:56.830
should raise a flag for you.

02:04:56.830 --> 02:04:59.460
And by that I mean a positive
flag because once again,

02:04:59.460 --> 02:05:02.430
we are back into the world and
therefore the neural circuits

02:05:02.430 --> 02:05:04.770
of movement and motion.

02:05:04.770 --> 02:05:08.340
So three elements of world
building perspective, shifting,

02:05:08.340 --> 02:05:10.950
and active generating are
what make up this narrative

02:05:10.950 --> 02:05:12.240
approach to creativity.

02:05:12.240 --> 02:05:14.640
And I should mention that
these authors and others are

02:05:14.640 --> 02:05:17.190
using such approach with
companies, with groups,

02:05:17.190 --> 02:05:18.003
with individuals.

02:05:18.003 --> 02:05:19.920
So this is using a bunch
of different contexts

02:05:19.920 --> 02:05:23.790
to approach and enhance
different forms of creativity.

02:05:23.790 --> 02:05:27.190
So let's talk first about
world building techniques.

02:05:27.190 --> 02:05:30.720
This is going to be
immediately familiar to you

02:05:30.720 --> 02:05:31.630
when you hear it.

02:05:31.630 --> 02:05:36.600
But one of the key elements of
creativity is to at the outset

02:05:36.600 --> 02:05:39.900
come up with some idea
that makes sense or is

02:05:39.900 --> 02:05:43.470
attractive to you about how
the world is different inside

02:05:43.470 --> 02:05:44.920
of your creative endeavor.

02:05:44.920 --> 02:05:47.328
So for those that
write science fiction

02:05:47.328 --> 02:05:48.870
or think about
science fiction, there

02:05:48.870 --> 02:05:50.820
are some obvious
aspects to this.

02:05:50.820 --> 02:05:52.710
But for those of you
that don't, maybe you

02:05:52.710 --> 02:05:54.340
come up with a
narrative, for instance,

02:05:54.340 --> 02:05:57.330
in the context of storytelling
that in your world

02:05:57.330 --> 02:06:01.045
we are the house
cats and the cats

02:06:01.045 --> 02:06:03.420
are actually the ones that
are the curators of the Earth.

02:06:03.420 --> 02:06:03.920
OK.

02:06:03.920 --> 02:06:06.810
So right there, there
is a conceptual shift

02:06:06.810 --> 02:06:10.020
that the world in which
whatever creative idea is going

02:06:10.020 --> 02:06:12.150
to emerge is entirely
different than the one

02:06:12.150 --> 02:06:13.320
that we actually live in.

02:06:13.320 --> 02:06:15.782
So that sets a certain number
of important constraints.

02:06:15.782 --> 02:06:17.490
It means certain things
are now possible,

02:06:17.490 --> 02:06:18.907
other things are
not possible that

02:06:18.907 --> 02:06:21.210
are very different from
the world that we live in.

02:06:21.210 --> 02:06:25.025
You can see the parallels here
to childhood imagination where

02:06:25.025 --> 02:06:27.150
essentially anything can
happen in the child's mind

02:06:27.150 --> 02:06:28.560
because they are unconstrained.

02:06:28.560 --> 02:06:30.270
The second element
is this perspective

02:06:30.270 --> 02:06:31.530
shifting techniques.

02:06:31.530 --> 02:06:35.210
And the idea here is
that not only are we

02:06:35.210 --> 02:06:38.990
supposed to have the reader or
the listener or the observer

02:06:38.990 --> 02:06:43.460
or us explore for creativity
and develop a creative idea

02:06:43.460 --> 02:06:46.968
by thinking differently
which is a generic term.

02:06:46.968 --> 02:06:48.510
How do we actually
think differently.

02:06:48.510 --> 02:06:52.340
But rather than just say take
the perspective of somebody

02:06:52.340 --> 02:06:54.500
else in terms of what
they would see or do

02:06:54.500 --> 02:06:57.350
or say or think
rather, we are supposed

02:06:57.350 --> 02:06:59.420
to think about their
underlying motivation.

02:06:59.420 --> 02:07:02.840
So we could do the world shift,
that is the world structure

02:07:02.840 --> 02:07:03.950
shift from step one.

02:07:03.950 --> 02:07:07.190
And then in step two, you
would ask yourself OK,

02:07:07.190 --> 02:07:10.370
rather than write about
or think about or move

02:07:10.370 --> 02:07:12.620
from the perspective
of myself, let's say,

02:07:12.620 --> 02:07:14.810
you're feeling particularly
happy that day.

02:07:14.810 --> 02:07:17.180
You'd say, I'm actually
going to take the perspective

02:07:17.180 --> 02:07:18.440
of somebody who's angry.

02:07:18.440 --> 02:07:20.717
But rather than
just act angry, I'm

02:07:20.717 --> 02:07:23.300
going to think about what their
motivation for being angry is.

02:07:23.300 --> 02:07:25.130
Maybe they had a breakup.

02:07:25.130 --> 02:07:26.210
Maybe they were jealous.

02:07:26.210 --> 02:07:28.240
Maybe somebody had
wronged them in some way.

02:07:28.240 --> 02:07:30.740
Maybe they're just generally
angry at the world for whatever

02:07:30.740 --> 02:07:33.770
reason and then operate from
that motivational stance.

02:07:33.770 --> 02:07:36.890
And this is a very
interesting and powerful step

02:07:36.890 --> 02:07:39.020
because what it really
captures at least

02:07:39.020 --> 02:07:41.060
as viewed by me,
the neuroscientists,

02:07:41.060 --> 02:07:43.580
is it captures a whole set
of neural circuits about what

02:07:43.580 --> 02:07:46.610
that motivational state means
because motivational states

02:07:46.610 --> 02:07:49.920
dictate a huge number of
possible different outcomes,

02:07:49.920 --> 02:07:53.010
but they really constrain the
number of different actions

02:07:53.010 --> 02:07:54.890
and outcomes that any
of us would engage in.

02:07:54.890 --> 02:07:57.140
Rather than saying I'm going
to view the world the way

02:07:57.140 --> 02:07:58.723
that someone else
will view the world,

02:07:58.723 --> 02:08:00.680
by stating that we are
going to be motivated

02:08:00.680 --> 02:08:03.170
by their set of motivations
and not our own,

02:08:03.170 --> 02:08:06.260
it includes a lot more
possibilities and yet not

02:08:06.260 --> 02:08:07.880
an infinite number
of possibilities.

02:08:07.880 --> 02:08:09.780
They are constrained
in a logical way,

02:08:09.780 --> 02:08:12.265
which is one of the key
elements of creativity.

02:08:12.265 --> 02:08:13.640
And then the third
element, which

02:08:13.640 --> 02:08:18.530
is action generating techniques
is a really cool one that you

02:08:18.530 --> 02:08:20.030
will immediately
notice implications

02:08:20.030 --> 02:08:23.390
for the workplace, which
is forced collaboration.

02:08:23.390 --> 02:08:25.820
So inside of this thing
that we're building here,

02:08:25.820 --> 02:08:30.350
this story, you create a
novel rule for the world

02:08:30.350 --> 02:08:32.060
that your story is
going to exist in

02:08:32.060 --> 02:08:36.050
or your music is going to exist
in or your sport will exist in.

02:08:36.050 --> 02:08:38.540
Then you create this
perspective shift

02:08:38.540 --> 02:08:41.270
where you take on the
motivation of someone

02:08:41.270 --> 02:08:43.640
else different than
you and then you

02:08:43.640 --> 02:08:46.220
force collaboration
between that person who

02:08:46.220 --> 02:08:49.280
has this alternate
motivation different from you

02:08:49.280 --> 02:08:51.740
and someone else who has an
entirely different motivation.

02:08:51.740 --> 02:08:53.570
And in doing so,
you create these

02:08:53.570 --> 02:08:55.642
what are called the
creative collisions.

02:08:55.642 --> 02:08:58.100
Now they're collisions because
they're crossing one another

02:08:58.100 --> 02:08:59.850
and something new has
to emerge from them.

02:08:59.850 --> 02:09:01.590
They could be antagonistic.

02:09:01.590 --> 02:09:03.860
They could be arguments
fighting physical or verbal

02:09:03.860 --> 02:09:06.000
or Otherwise they
could be synergistic.

02:09:06.000 --> 02:09:08.000
They could take on any
number of different forms

02:09:08.000 --> 02:09:10.490
depending on the motivations
and the individuals that

02:09:10.490 --> 02:09:11.460
are involved.

02:09:11.460 --> 02:09:13.490
But even though I
just described this

02:09:13.490 --> 02:09:16.220
in fairly top contour,
what I just described

02:09:16.220 --> 02:09:19.760
is actually the core
elements of any story

02:09:19.760 --> 02:09:20.890
or any creative endeavor.

02:09:20.890 --> 02:09:23.390
It's just that many stories are
from the perspective of what

02:09:23.390 --> 02:09:27.530
we already know and believe
and think the world to be.

02:09:27.530 --> 02:09:31.340
And our own perspective
and the actions

02:09:31.340 --> 02:09:34.130
that we would take given that
world in that perspective.

02:09:34.130 --> 02:09:36.330
Whereas if we want
to be creative,

02:09:36.330 --> 02:09:38.750
we want to think outside
of our usual framework

02:09:38.750 --> 02:09:41.240
and yet using elements
that exist within us

02:09:41.240 --> 02:09:43.220
no one has to tell us
the creative narrative.

02:09:43.220 --> 02:09:45.120
We're trying to come
up with it on our own.

02:09:45.120 --> 02:09:47.930
We want to essentially
think in a childlike way

02:09:47.930 --> 02:09:49.160
how do children think?

02:09:49.160 --> 02:09:53.570
Well, they have new
different or entirely novel

02:09:53.570 --> 02:09:55.370
concepts about how
the world works.

02:09:55.370 --> 02:09:57.202
But those are bounded.

02:09:57.202 --> 02:09:58.160
And this is a key word.

02:09:58.160 --> 02:09:58.910
Those are bounded.

02:09:58.910 --> 02:09:59.785
They're not infinite.

02:09:59.785 --> 02:10:01.940
It's not that
anything can happen.

02:10:01.940 --> 02:10:04.482
Some kids will say, you can fly
and you can shoot lasers out

02:10:04.482 --> 02:10:05.940
of your eyes, you
can do all things

02:10:05.940 --> 02:10:08.150
that are unicorns or candy
falling from the sky.

02:10:08.150 --> 02:10:12.600
At some point, if you don't
bound the change in the world,

02:10:12.600 --> 02:10:15.180
it just becomes pure chaos and
even children don't do that.

02:10:15.180 --> 02:10:17.450
So we need to bound the
change and yet create

02:10:17.450 --> 02:10:21.260
some alternate universe, if you
will, in which the story takes

02:10:21.260 --> 02:10:23.625
place or the
creation of any kind,

02:10:23.625 --> 02:10:25.250
doesn't have to be
a story takes place.

02:10:25.250 --> 02:10:26.810
Then there has to be
a perspective shift.

02:10:26.810 --> 02:10:27.810
And this is very useful.

02:10:27.810 --> 02:10:30.050
This is actually a
tool that we can all

02:10:30.050 --> 02:10:32.270
use of trying to take
the perspective of others

02:10:32.270 --> 02:10:35.990
but not just asking what they
would feel or think or do.

02:10:35.990 --> 02:10:39.740
But ask what is their
motivation in life generally

02:10:39.740 --> 02:10:43.170
or what mood stance or goal
stance are they taking.

02:10:43.170 --> 02:10:44.840
Are they trying to
extract from others?

02:10:44.840 --> 02:10:46.280
Are they trying
to give to others?

02:10:46.280 --> 02:10:48.470
Are they very altruistic,
et cetera, et cetera.

02:10:48.470 --> 02:10:50.300
And then you take
that individual

02:10:50.300 --> 02:10:53.125
and you do that also for
another individual or group

02:10:53.125 --> 02:10:54.500
of individuals
and then you start

02:10:54.500 --> 02:10:57.597
thinking about how those
different individuals because

02:10:57.597 --> 02:10:59.180
of their different
motivational states

02:10:59.180 --> 02:11:00.873
would engage at the
level of action.

02:11:00.873 --> 02:11:02.540
What they would do,
what they would say,

02:11:02.540 --> 02:11:03.957
would they mate,
would they fight,

02:11:03.957 --> 02:11:05.600
would they-- et
cetera, et cetera.

02:11:05.600 --> 02:11:06.770
You think of any story.

02:11:06.770 --> 02:11:09.740
The story of Star Wars,
of the Greek myths.

02:11:09.740 --> 02:11:14.210
You think of any story that has
been created, which we consider

02:11:14.210 --> 02:11:16.190
great and novel works.

02:11:16.190 --> 02:11:20.270
And you start to find these
three elements-- worldbuilding,

02:11:20.270 --> 02:11:23.360
perspective shifting, and
action generating techniques.

02:11:23.360 --> 02:11:27.590
And so while this is, again,
just a broad contour of what

02:11:27.590 --> 02:11:29.240
this narrative
approach involves,

02:11:29.240 --> 02:11:31.430
I think it's a very
important and very exciting

02:11:31.430 --> 02:11:33.425
one because it
gives us a formula.

02:11:33.425 --> 02:11:35.050
We already know that
divergent thinking

02:11:35.050 --> 02:11:36.758
and convergent thinking
are both elements

02:11:36.758 --> 02:11:38.440
of the creative process.

02:11:38.440 --> 02:11:41.170
This is suggesting
that whether or not

02:11:41.170 --> 02:11:43.072
it involves divergent
thinking or not,

02:11:43.072 --> 02:11:44.530
these authors seem
to think this is

02:11:44.530 --> 02:11:46.960
distinct from divergent
thinking that capturing some

02:11:46.960 --> 02:11:49.960
of the elements of creativity
that are present in childhood

02:11:49.960 --> 02:11:51.910
but that then tend to
disappear as we start

02:11:51.910 --> 02:11:54.820
to assume identity, build
identity, and understand rules

02:11:54.820 --> 02:11:56.560
about the actual
world we live in.

02:11:56.560 --> 02:12:00.340
All of those basic elements
of early childhood creativity

02:12:00.340 --> 02:12:01.343
can be reawakened.

02:12:01.343 --> 02:12:03.760
And, in fact, they have data
to support the fact that they

02:12:03.760 --> 02:12:06.190
can be reawakened in
adults in meaningful ways

02:12:06.190 --> 02:12:08.620
that can lead to new product
design, new workplace

02:12:08.620 --> 02:12:10.600
interactions, and on and on.

02:12:10.600 --> 02:12:12.680
That I find very exciting.

02:12:12.680 --> 02:12:15.730
And as a consequence,
I do intend

02:12:15.730 --> 02:12:18.610
to do an entire episode
at some point on narrative

02:12:18.610 --> 02:12:19.480
and storytelling.

02:12:19.480 --> 02:12:21.340
And the role of narrative
and storytelling

02:12:21.340 --> 02:12:23.230
not just for sake of
creativity but also

02:12:23.230 --> 02:12:27.460
for accessing neuroplasticity
and for enhancing memory

02:12:27.460 --> 02:12:28.010
and so on.

02:12:28.010 --> 02:12:30.550
There's an entire landscape of
literature and exciting tools

02:12:30.550 --> 02:12:31.850
and things to understand there.

02:12:31.850 --> 02:12:34.870
But in the meantime, we will
provide a link to this paper.

02:12:34.870 --> 02:12:37.450
And for those of you that
choose not to access the paper,

02:12:37.450 --> 02:12:40.960
simply understanding these
three aspects of narrative

02:12:40.960 --> 02:12:43.180
as an alternative to
accessing creativity.

02:12:43.180 --> 02:12:47.770
That is, a dedicated and well
understood or established

02:12:47.770 --> 02:12:51.070
world shift that you choose,
perspective shifting and taking

02:12:51.070 --> 02:12:54.370
on the motivation of
others, and creating

02:12:54.370 --> 02:12:57.490
some landscape of exploration
for what interactions would

02:12:57.490 --> 02:12:59.740
occur between that individual
or groups of individuals

02:12:59.740 --> 02:13:02.650
and other individuals that
have other motivations

02:13:02.650 --> 02:13:05.860
and yet are still living
in this alternate world.

02:13:05.860 --> 02:13:08.290
Those three elements
we now know can

02:13:08.290 --> 02:13:11.530
be combined into what
you or I or anyone

02:13:11.530 --> 02:13:13.970
would consider important
creative works.

02:13:13.970 --> 02:13:16.270
So today we
discussed creativity.

02:13:16.270 --> 02:13:19.630
This absolutely fascinating
aspect to human brain

02:13:19.630 --> 02:13:23.830
function that has allowed us as
a species to develop everything

02:13:23.830 --> 02:13:25.870
from great works
of art and music

02:13:25.870 --> 02:13:29.590
to technological innovations
that allow us to fly and allow

02:13:29.590 --> 02:13:32.230
us to access people all over
the world through little screen

02:13:32.230 --> 02:13:36.615
devices that we carry around
in our pockets and on and on.

02:13:36.615 --> 02:13:38.740
As I mentioned at the
beginning of today's episode,

02:13:38.740 --> 02:13:42.550
I find creativity to be one of
the most fascinating aspects

02:13:42.550 --> 02:13:43.960
of brain function.

02:13:43.960 --> 02:13:46.810
And in particular,
because we don't actually

02:13:46.810 --> 02:13:48.580
know what the upper
limits of creativity

02:13:48.580 --> 02:13:52.778
are and yet we understand
that there are certain bounds,

02:13:52.778 --> 02:13:54.070
there are certain requirements.

02:13:54.070 --> 02:13:56.110
And the key requirement
for creativity

02:13:56.110 --> 02:13:57.700
is this aspect of utility.

02:13:57.700 --> 02:13:59.200
Now, that doesn't
necessarily mean

02:13:59.200 --> 02:14:01.370
that for something to
be considered creative

02:14:01.370 --> 02:14:04.180
it has to be useful in
the practical sense,

02:14:04.180 --> 02:14:06.130
but it does seem
that for something

02:14:06.130 --> 02:14:10.000
to be considered truly
creative or especially creative

02:14:10.000 --> 02:14:12.190
in some cases that
it revealed to us

02:14:12.190 --> 02:14:16.338
something fundamental about the
way that we or the world works.

02:14:16.338 --> 02:14:18.130
We discuss some of the
neural circuits that

02:14:18.130 --> 02:14:20.140
underlie the different
aspects of creativity

02:14:20.140 --> 02:14:22.180
in particular divergent
and convergent thinking

02:14:22.180 --> 02:14:24.970
as well as narrative building
and some of the tools

02:14:24.970 --> 02:14:27.880
and steps that can allow
us to better access

02:14:27.880 --> 02:14:30.310
divergent thinking and
convergent thinking.

02:14:30.310 --> 02:14:33.130
And those tools include
behavioral tools as well as

02:14:33.130 --> 02:14:34.330
pharmacology.

02:14:34.330 --> 02:14:38.470
And we talked about narrative
building as a way to reawaken

02:14:38.470 --> 02:14:41.830
or I should say, reaccess
the childhood creativity

02:14:41.830 --> 02:14:45.520
that did indeed exist in all
of us at some point in time.

